window.__hvCxRegister("alc1", {"v":1,"dialogue":"alc1","title":"Alcibiades I","speechLabel":"On this part of the dialogue","eras":[{"id":"early-empire","label":"Early Roman Empire"},{"id":"late-antiquity","label":"Late antiquity"},{"id":"byzantine","label":"Byzantium"},{"id":"renaissance","label":"Renaissance"},{"id":"modern","label":"Modern"}],"items":[{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":2,"ref":"","t":"“Son of Clinias! you wonder, I suppose”","x":"The prefaces (τα μροοιμια) of Plato’s dialogues accord with the whole scope of them; and are neither devised by Plato for the sake of dramatic allurement, since this mode of writing is very remote from the magnitude of the …","tier":"passage","r":[[514,516]],"rs":"103a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":7,"ref":"","t":"“You have looked down on your admirers”","x":"Alcibiades, by despising those that were unworthy of his love, and admiring those that were worthy of it, testifies his great alliance to the beautiful, and that he was abundantly prepared for the reception of virtue. But perhaps …","tier":"passage","r":[[520,522]],"rs":"104b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":9,"ref":"","t":"“You think that if you speedily make your appearance before the Athenian”","x":"The design of all that has been proximately said is to purify our dianoëtic part from two-fold ignorance, and to remove all that impedes our resumption of true science. For it is impossible for things imperfect to obtain their …","tier":"passage","r":[[525,527]],"rs":"105b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":11,"ref":"","t":"“In fine, that you entertain such hopes as I have mentioned, I…”","x":"From hence we may rightly understand who it is that instructs, and who is instructed. For it is fit that he who instructs should accurately know the aptitudes of those that are instructed, and conformably to these should direct …","tier":"passage","r":[[526,528]],"rs":"105c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":23,"ref":"","t":"“Now what the things are which you have learnt I tolerably well…”","x":"That these three disciplines, says Proclus, viz. mathesis, music, and gymnastic, contribute to the whole of political virtue, is evident. For gymnastic strengthens the softness of desire, and recalls its dissipated nature to a …","tier":"passage","r":[[533,535]],"rs":"106e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":27,"ref":"","t":"“How is this, friend Alcibiades”","x":"The descent of the soul into body separates it from divine souls, from whom it is filled with intelligence, power, and purity, and conjoins it with generation, and nature, and material things, from which it is filled with …","tier":"passage","r":[[547,549]],"rs":"109d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":38,"ref":"","t":"“But, by Jove, I was not ignorant of that point; for I…”","x":"The young man here acknowledges that he has a suspicion of the knowledge of things just; and hence Socrates again asks him whether he learnt or discovered this knowledge. In consequence of this, Alcibiades confesses, that his …","tier":"passage","r":[[551,553]],"rs":"110c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":65,"ref":"","t":"“In those assertions, was it not said that Alcibiades”","x":"It is rightly said by the Stoics, that the man who is void of erudition accuses others, and not himself, as the causes of his infelicity; but that he who has made some advances in knowledge refers to himself the cause of all that …","tier":"passage","r":[[565,567]],"rs":"113b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":70,"ref":"","t":"“For I suppose that justice and interest are not the same thing”","x":"The dogma, that the just is the same with the profitable, contains the whole of moral philosophy. For those who suppose these to be different, must necessarily admit, that felicity receives its completion from externals; since …","tier":"passage","r":[[567,569]],"rs":"113d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":72,"ref":"","t":"“Suppose interest to be a thing ever so different from justice”","x":"Proclus in commenting on this part observes, that souls express the forms of those things to which they conjoin themselves.","tier":"passage","r":[[568,570]],"rs":"113e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":75,"ref":"","t":"“Why, my good friend, suppose me to be the assembly and the…”","x":"Proclus here observes, that it is the province of the same science to persuade one person and many; which assertion is, as it were, a certain hymn and encomium of science. For the great excellence of science is evident in this …","tier":"passage","r":[[570,572]],"rs":"114b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":78,"ref":"","t":"“Tell me then, do you say, that some just actions are advantageous”","x":"The proposed inquiry concerning things just and profitable, whether they are the same, or are divided from each other according to the diversity of subjects, contributes to the whole of philosophy, and adheres to the whole truth …","tier":"passage","r":[[573,575]],"rs":"114e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":98,"ref":"","t":"“But what as to actions which are beautiful”","x":"The multitude falsely think that wounds and death are evils. For what among these can be an evil to us whose essence consists in soul? since neither do the passions of instruments change the virtues of those that use them. …","tier":"passage","r":[[574,576]],"rs":"115a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":0,"ref":"Lecture 1","t":"The goal of the Alcibiades according to Proclus: seven supporting arguments","x":"Now then, the target (skopos) of the present dialogue, according to Proclus, is self-knowledge: and this is confirmed by a plurality of arguments.","tier":"dialogue","r":[[515,679]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":28,"ref":"Lecture 2","t":"The usefulness (khrêsimon) of the dialogue","x":"After [our discussion of] the target, which is threefold – including the views adopted by Proclus, by Damascius, and by the commentators (i.e. the view shared by the commentators) – come, let us also get into detail about the …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[515,679]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":46,"ref":"Lecture 3","t":"“Son of Clinias, I think that you are wondering…”","x":"Because the dialogue is about love (erôtikos), [Plato] uses the proem to explain three differences between the divinely inspired (entheos) lover and the crude (phortikos) lover.","tier":"passage","r":[[515,516]],"rs":"103a–103b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":142,"ref":"Lecture 4","t":"“Now I have been making a study of you…”","x":"Socrates is making two points here, one of which is human, the other divine. Naturally it is human to be in love, since this is common to all human beings: but it is divine to love without being [physically] present …","tier":"passage","r":[[516,522]],"rs":"103b–104c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":173,"ref":"Lecture 5","t":"“As a matter of fact, Socrates, perhaps you don’t…”","x":"Since we have finished learning from Socrates’ words of reason (logoi), come, let us examine Alcibiades’ utterances (phônai) too. Now, it is appropriate that we have attributed ‘words of reason’ to Socrates, but ‘utterances’ to …","tier":"passage","r":[[522,527]],"rs":"104c–105c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":192,"ref":"Lecture 5","t":"The god questions Alcibiades: 105A–C","x":"But come, let us look into the god’s questions, and the philosopher’s answers to the god on behalf of Alcibiades, [and consider] what lessons (dogmata) we should take away from the god’s questions and the philosopher’s answers. …","tier":"passage","r":[[525,527]],"rs":"105a–105c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":212,"ref":"Lecture 6","t":"“what’s this got to do with your point (logos)?…”","x":"Two [questions] had been advanced in the proem [103A–104E]: first, why does Socrates love [Alcibiades]? And second, why does Socrates accompany him by loving in silence (that is, why does he love him without being present with …","tier":"passage","r":[[528,532]],"rs":"105d–106c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":245,"ref":"Lecture 7","t":"“Come on, then: I say that you intend to…”","x":"There are three basic components of being a good adviser, as follows: good intentions (prohairesis agathê), exact knowledge (gnôsis akribês), and expressive ability (dunamis apangeltikê) (for the adviser must have goodwill for …","tier":"passage","r":[[532,536]],"rs":"106c–107b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":300,"ref":"Lecture 8","t":"“Then what will they be considering when you stand…”","x":"Again Alcibiades, who is a naturally gift ed individual (euphuês) disposed toward oratory, evades Socrates’ questions [107C]. For once Socrates has demonstrated that he is not worth much as an adviser, neither about [1] subjects …","tier":"passage","r":[[537,543]],"rs":"107c–108d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":324,"ref":"Lecture 9","t":"“Come on, now, what do you call what’s better…”","x":"Alcibiades has been asked to state the goal of war [108D], and he ought to answer ‘justice’. We go to war for the sake of justice, just as doctors tackle illnesses for the sake of health, that is, in order to bring the elements …","tier":"passage","r":[[543,553]],"rs":"108d–110d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":349,"ref":"Lecture 10","t":"“But maybe I gave you the wrong answer, when…”","x":"Now that Alcibiades has been proven ignorant of justice (since he neither learned it nor found it out, as [Socrates] demonstrated earlier), he uses his natural talents to reply, by rejecting the premiss that he knows about …","tier":"passage","r":[[553,563]],"rs":"110d–112d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":384,"ref":"Lecture 11","t":"“Well, then, given that your opinion wavers so much…”","x":"Because Alcibiades has been proven doubly ignorant about justice (for he","tier":"passage","r":[[563,571]],"rs":"112d–114b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":451,"ref":"Lecture 12","t":"“But I don’t know if I’d be able to…”","x":"Due to his natural talent, Alcibiades has withdrawn his endorsement of the","tier":"passage","r":[[571,575]],"rs":"114b–115a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":492,"ref":"Lecture 13","t":"“Now tell me: are you saying that some just…”","x":"The present dialogue has ten syllogisms; he lays out the fifth of these to prove that the just and the advantageous are one and the same. He uses two syllogisms to demonstrate this: first, that everything just is also …","tier":"passage","r":[[575,581]],"rs":"115a–116b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":513,"ref":"Lecture 14","t":"“Now then, let’s take a new approach: people who…”","x":"Since Socrates demonstrated that everything noble is good, using the example of courage (since he was explaining how ‘the noble, insofar as it is noble, is desirable; everything desirable is good; therefore, everything noble is …","tier":"passage","r":[[581,591]],"rs":"116b–118b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":540,"ref":"Lecture 15","t":"“Oh dear, Alcibiades, what suffering (pathos) you’ve been through”","x":"Consider that there are two parts of the soul, one concerned with cognition, the other with vital functions: the cognitive part is purified by cross-examination, but the vital part is purified by forceful correction (epiplêxis). …","tier":"passage","r":[[591,595]],"rs":"118b–119a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":559,"ref":"Lecture 16","t":"“All right, then. What’s your plan for yourself? To…”","x":"During the first section [of the dialogue, 106C–119A], Socrates cross-examined and refuted (elenxas) Alcibiades, proving for the cognitive [part of his soul] that he is doubly ignorant, and using tragic flair to explain to the …","tier":"passage","r":[[595,603]],"rs":"119a–120d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":614,"ref":"Lecture 17","t":"“Is it likely that natural talents will be greater…”","x":"Following Socrates’ guidance, Alcibiades should have regarded his competitors as worthy of consideration, even if they were not really so, in order that he might take great care to strive for excellence. But since he is lazy …","tier":"passage","r":[[603,611]],"rs":"120d–122b"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":633,"ref":"Lecture 18","t":"“And again, if you care to consider the wealth…”","x":"Socrates has compared Alcibiades with the Persian King and proven him inferior to that man [i.e., the King], arguing from four distinct [bases]: [1] from homeland or lineage, [2] from birth, which he called ‘birthday …","tier":"passage","r":[[611,620]],"rs":"122b–124a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":676,"ref":"Lecture 19","t":"“No, my blessed friend, trust in me and in…”","x":"Now the first and second part of the refutations of Alcibiades initiated a shared","tier":"passage","r":[[620,623]],"rs":"124a–124d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":706,"ref":"Lecture 20","t":"“Now tell me: we say that we want to…”","x":"Now since the target (skopos) of the dialogue is knowledge of ourselves","tier":"passage","r":[[623,632]],"rs":"124d–126c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":774,"ref":"Lecture 21","t":"“When you say ‘friendship’ (philia), do you mean agreement…”","x":"Since Alcibiades was describing friendship as the goal of the statesman’s knowledge, Socrates challenges him – not because he himself disagrees that friendship is the goal, but because he suspected that Alcibiades did not …","tier":"passage","r":[[632,639]],"rs":"126c–127e"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":806,"ref":"Lecture 22","t":"“When you say ‘friendship’ (philia), do you mean agreement…”","x":"Since Alcibiades described friendship as the goal of the statesman’s knowledge,","tier":"passage","r":[[632,639]],"rs":"126c–127e"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":828,"ref":"Lecture 23","t":"“Come then, what is it to cultivate or care…”","x":"Since Socrates’ speech has now arrived at the necessity of exercising great care [for one’s own cultivation], [therefore], before he teaches what the method (tropos) of cultivation is, he presents in advance two premisses …","tier":"passage","r":[[639,645]],"rs":"127e–129a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":861,"ref":"Lecture 24","t":"“Come then, in what way might the self itself…”","x":"The object proposed is to find out what our being (ousia) is, since a human being (anthrôpos) is neither the body nor the combination [of soul and body]. And he demonstrates this through two syllogisms, [A] one categorical and …","tier":"passage","r":[[646,650]],"rs":"129b–130a"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":915,"ref":"Lecture 26","t":"“Come then, in what way might the self itself…”","x":"He describes the rational soul as ‘the self itself’, not as it uses the body as an instrument or dwells in a body, like the purificatory or contemplative person: and he describes the rational soul as ‘self’ when it avails itself …","tier":"passage","r":[[646,652]],"rs":"129b–130c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":901,"ref":"Lecture 25","t":"“Then the soul rules (arkhousa)? – Yes. – In…”","x":"Socrates, having demonstrated through a categorical syllogism [A, above] that","tier":"passage","r":[[650,653]],"rs":"130a–130d"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":932,"ref":"Lecture 27","t":"“So the right way of looking at it is…”","x":"As for ‘the conversation of Socrates with Alcibiades’ [130E], [Socrates] made its introduction out of dialectical struggles, and its conclusion using intoxication with love. For restoration or return (apokatastasis) is also …","tier":"passage","r":[[653,667]],"rs":"130d–133c"},{"c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":946,"ref":"Lecture 28","t":"“But we agree that knowing oneself is the same…”","x":"Since we got to know ourselves as a civic person (as we recognised that the human being is a soul using the body as an instrument), and as a purificatory person (as we recognised that it is a rational soul making no use of the …","tier":"passage","r":[[667,679]],"rs":"133c–135e"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":1,"ref":"","t":"Introduction to the First Alcibiades","x":"The most peculiar and firm principle, says Proclus, of all the dialogues of Plato, and of the whole theory of that philosopher, is the knowledge of our own nature; for, this being properly established as an hypothesis, we shall …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[515,679]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"}],"rk":"alc1","glosses":[{"s":515,"ri":-1,"ph":"you are wondering what exactly I have in mind","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":4,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads Alcibiades' wonder about Socrates' purpose as the first step of conversion: wishing to learn the cause of Socrates' conduct already makes him a lover of Socratic science.","q":"For it leads Alcibiades from a life tending to externals to a survey of himself, and recalls him through a knowledge of himself to a love of Socratic science; since a desire to learn the cause of the conduct of Socrates is to become a lover of the pre-subsisting science which he contains."},{"s":515,"ri":-1,"ph":"you have prevailed over your lovers while they, in their inferiority, have been dominated","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":8,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes Alcibiades' scorn for unworthy lovers as evidence of his kinship with the beautiful and of a nature well prepared to receive virtue.","q":"Alcibiades, by despising those that were unworthy of his love, and admiring those that were worthy of it, testifies his great alliance to the beautiful, and that he was abundantly prepared for the reception of virtue."},{"s":515,"ri":-1,"ph":"it seems to me that you are much less proud of that","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus takes this to mean that Alcibiades is not proud of his wealth at all and does not revere himself for it, because he is no lover of money.","q":"For he means that 'you aren't proud at all of your wealth, since that you don't revere yourself over this, because you don't care about money ( philokhrêmatos )'."},{"s":515,"ri":-1,"ph":"None of this has escaped your notice","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus says these words turn Alcibiades back toward himself and toward self-knowledge, serving the dialogue's target, and echo the opening 'I think that you wonder'.","q":"With these words, Socrates turns Alcibiades back toward himself and toward self-knowledge, acting to the advantage of the dialogue's target ( skopos )."},{"s":515,"ri":-1,"ph":"I know quite well that you are wondering","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":170,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus notes that the opening 'I think' has become 'I know well' now that the demonstrative arguments are complete.","q":"And it is certainly fair to wonder at how, at the beginning (prior to the demonstrative arguments) he made the phrase 'you wonder' less certain by adding 'I think', but now (after the demonstrative arguments) he said 'I know well', unambiguously"},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"Son of Clinias","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":4,"ref":"","n":"Proclus holds that this opening is no mere dramatic ornament: it signals the scope of the whole dialogue, turning the young man towards himself and so towards the Delphic command to know himself.","q":"Agreeably to this, Plato in the very beginning of this dialogue appears to me to indicate in a beautiful manner the scope of the whole composition."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"I alone have not departed now that the others have gone","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":4,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that asking why Socrates alone of the lovers loved first and never changed his love makes Alcibiades a spectator of the whole life of Socrates.","q":"For an investigation of the cause through which Socrates alone, of all his lovers, does not change his love, but began to love him prior to others, and is not altered when the rest no longer love, evinces him to be a spectator of the whole life of Socrates."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"I think you are amazed","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":6,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the tentative 'I suppose' (oimai): since Socrates speaks of an unstable nature, the word marks the mutability of the thing known, not any vagueness in Socrates' knowledge.","q":"is it wonderful that Socrates, who is here discoursing concerning an unstable nature, should use the word οιμαι, I opine, or suppose? which signifies that the nature of the thing known is mutable, but does not accuse the knowledge of Socrates as indefinite, mingled with ignorance, and dubious."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"Son of Clinias","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":124,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus gives three readings of the patronymic: an archaic usage, a nod to Alcibiades' pride in his father, who fell at Coronea, or a lively form of address suited to lovers.","q":"Son of Clinias. This can be interpreted in three ways. (1) Either the patronymic ( hê ek patros klêsis ) is used as an archaism"},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"you are amazed","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":49,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus takes the opening as marking the first difference between lovers: the crude lover wonders at his beloved, whereas the inspired lover is the object of the beloved's wonder.","q":"The first difference is that the crude lover wonders at his beloved ( ta paidika ), whereas the divinely inspired lover is the object of [his beloved's] wonder."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"I think you are amazed","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":129,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus asks whether the tentative 'I think' shows ignorance, and answers that it shows the highest knowledge: Socrates approaches the young man cautiously, as bird-catchers approach birds.","q":"Not at all, but instead [that it indicates] the highest [level of] knowledge, to approach [different] personalities ( prosôpois ) using a method that is appropriate [to each]."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"you are amazed","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":129,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus finds the word suited to the dialogue's target, since wonder is the beginning of philosophy, leading from the fact that something is to the cause why it is.","q":"The phrase is suitable to the target ( skopos ) [of the dialogue], as wonder is 'the beginning of philosophy'."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"being your first lover, I alone have not departed now that the others have gone","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":50,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus sees the second difference here: the crude lover abandons the beloved when youth fades, while the inspired lover stays from cradle to grave, before, alongside and after the others.","q":"indicating that he follows his beloved before they do, alongside them, and after them."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"although the others conversed with you in droves","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":129,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates calls himself one, first and only, but the others many and a mob, the mob ranking below the people and the people below the chorus.","q":"He uses the words 'one' ( heis ) and 'first' ( prôtos ) and 'only' ( monos ) of himself, but 'multitude' ( polloi ) and 'last' ( teleutaioi ) and 'mob' ( okhlos ) of the others."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"I, in contrast, have not addressed you for so many years","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":51,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus reads the silence as the third difference: the inspired lover is present in a godlike way, absent insofar as he is silent but present insofar as he follows and loves.","q":"showing that he is absent insofar as he is silent, but present insofar as he follows and loves [Alcibiades]."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"for so many years","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":130,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus calls 'in so many years' an Attic figure of speech, meaning simply that Socrates kept silent for a long time.","q":"It is an Attic figure [of speech] ( skhêma ) to say 'in so many years' ( tosoutôn etôn ). This means that 'I said nothing for such a long time'."},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"The cause of this was nothing human","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":130,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus specifies that the cause is not of love simply but of godlike love without bodily presence, and that a godlike effect must have a divine cause.","q":"Clearly, of loving without [physical] presence. For it is not the cause of love unqualifiedly ( haplôs ), but of godlike love"},{"s":515,"ri":0,"ph":"being your first lover","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":7,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, notes that the dialogue begins from Socrates' love, and takes the amatory science to predominate throughout, with the dialectic and maieutic sciences mingled into it.","q":"Again, the amatory form of life is particularly indicated by Socrates in this dialogue. For the beginning is made from hence; and he proceeds perfecting the young man till he renders him a lover of his providential care, which is the leading good of the amatory art."},{"s":516,"ri":0,"ph":"it was a spiritual opposition","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":56,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus says the mention of the daimonic obliges commentators to expound the theory of daimons, which he divides into daimons by analogy, by essence and by relation.","q":"Because the 'daimonic' was mentioned in this passage, the commentators have been obliged to pay close attention to the theory ( logos ) of daimons."},{"s":516,"ri":0,"ph":"later you will learn about its power","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":130,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus says the promise is aimed at the listener: knowing Alcibiades is after power, Socrates leads him to expect talk of what is dear to his heart.","q":"because [Socrates] knows that [Alcibiades] is after power, he causes [Alcibiades] to expect that he will discuss matters dear to his own heart ( oikeios autôi ), namely, what he wants."},{"s":516,"ri":0,"ph":"since it is no longer opposing me","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":137,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus asks why the daimon no longer opposes, given Alcibiades' later misdeeds, and prefers the answer that Socrates' words would benefit him in another life.","q":"But the third solution is actually truer, that even if the [discussions] of Socrates were not going to benefit him in this life, then nevertheless in another life ( en heterôi biôi ) he would recollect these and be benefited"},{"s":516,"ri":0,"ph":"I have come to you in this way","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":139,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus asks how Socrates can say he has come when he was always present, and answers that 'I have come' refers to perceptible presence, not his former purely godlike presence.","q":"Well, we reply that 'I have come' evidently refers to his perceptible [presence] and not, as before, his solely godlike [presence], which also lacked any [perceptible] presence."},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"your exalted notions","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":8,"ref":"","n":"Proclus asks why such lofty ambitions attract divine lovers, and answers that despising present things as shadows and seeking something great shows the soul in labour with the beautiful.","q":"For to despise things present as shadowy representations, and of no worth, and to investigate something prior to these, which is great and wonderful and transcends the conceptions of the multitude, is an evident argument of the parturiency of the soul about the beautiful."},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"despite being numerous and high-minded","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":168,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus asks why crude lovers are called great-minded, and answers that great-mindedness, like Love, shares itself even with its opposite through abundance of power.","q":"likewise, here too we say that greatmindedness ( megalophrosunê ) has also provided a share of itself to its opposite, with the result that even the crude lovers are called 'great-minded'."},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"there is not one of them who has not fled, outdone by your superiority","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates chooses words such as great-minded, overwhelmed and fled because they suit the statesman and general Alcibiades hopes to become.","q":"For here he says 'great-minded', then 'overwhelmed by your spirit', and again 'have fled', and each and every one of these [phrases] suits the statesman ( politikos )."},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"I wish to explain the reason for your exalted notions","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates treats Alcibiades' scorn as having a reason, not empty conceit, so as to accustom him to a life lived according to a cause.","q":"Socrates uses these words to acclimatise the young man to a life lived with purpose ( kat' aitian )."},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"You claim not to need a single other person for any purpose","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":153,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus hears 'as you say' as 'you say it, not I', a refutation mixed with praise, like Euripides' 'You hear this from yourself, and not from me.'","q":"For again, [Socrates] remarks at the beginning of his praises, ‘as you say’ [sc. ‘that you need no man for anything’, 104A], and by this he means, ‘as you say and not I ’"},{"s":517,"ri":0,"ph":"not to need a single other person for any purpose","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":155,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus finds a hint of rashness here: needing nothing lies beyond human reach, above all for Alcibiades, who craves reputation and watches everyone for praise; only a god needs nothing.","q":"for through these [words], he hints at Alcibiades’ rashness, since this [sc. complete selfsuffi ciency] lies beyond the weak reach of human beings, and especially of Alcibiades, who is fond of his reputation and always keeping an eye on everyone [for praise]. For only a god needs nothing."},{"s":518,"ri":0,"ph":"beginning with the body and ending with the soul","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":157,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus sees a refutation in this order: Alcibiades' pride starts from bodily goods and ends with those of the soul, honouring the body first.","q":"And fourth, that 'your pride starts with bodily goods and ends with the [goods] of the soul, which was a mistake."},{"s":518,"ri":0,"ph":"you are supreme in beauty and greatness","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":162,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades' pride in beauty rests on a true notion: he glimpsed intelligible beauty but, unable to reach it, fought over its perceptible shadow.","q":"We say, then, that when he was boasting about beauty, he had intelligible beauty in his mind, and he held onto an image ( phantasia ) of it, but since he lacked the power to obtain it truly, he started 'fighting over shadows'"},{"s":518,"ri":0,"ph":"it is plain for all to see that you are not lying","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus finds a covert refutation: the true good is beyond words, so a good plain for everyone to see is base and not truly good.","q":"And if now he says, concerning [the good that is] present to Alcibiades, that it is 'clear to everyone to see', it is plain that this is a base thing and not truly 'good', so that even in these [words] there is a refutation."},{"s":519,"ri":0,"ph":"you belong to the most powerful family in your own city","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":160,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus finds a refutation in the boast of lineage: by Alcibiades' own standard, his ancestor twenty-five generations back might have been a slave.","q":"'If we adopt your standards for self-reverence, it's possible that your ancestor was a slave twenty-five generations back.'"},{"s":519,"ri":0,"ph":"numerous excellent friends and kindred who would serve your every need","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":160,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus sees a refutation in treating friends as servants, since friendships are equalities and a friend is another self.","q":"Another refutation comes when he remarks that 'You think your friends are your servants': for if, as the Pythagoreans have it, 'friendships are equalities', and according to Aristotle 'a friend is another self', why should [friends] be our 'servants' any more than we ourselves are?"},{"s":520,"ri":0,"ph":"in the person of Pericles, the son of Xanthippus","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":160,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus says pride in Pericles is mistaken, since the Gorgias shows Pericles was no true statesman and made the citizens worse.","q":"And 'you are mistaken to be proud of Pericles as a guardian', since this man had been shown in the Gorgias not to be a [true] statesman ( politikos )."},{"s":520,"ri":0,"ph":"you think you possess a greater power than all of those I have mentioned","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":169,"ref":"Lecture 4","n":"Olympiodorus notes that 'power' is language fit for a would-be general, used so that Socrates can lead him to learn that true power is knowledge.","q":"So he is all but shouting aloud, 'Learn what is true power: knowledge ( epistêmê )!'"},{"s":523,"ri":1,"ph":"you are just a little ahead of me","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":180,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus takes this as Alcibiades' wonder at Socrates' good timing, possible because the divine leads the human and the actual precedes the potential.","q":"[1] Now [Alcibiades wonders at] the good timing of [Socrates'] words when he says this: 'You just beat me to the punch'"},{"s":523,"ri":1,"ph":"what exactly do you want","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":182,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus says that asking what Socrates wants expresses wonder at his Good-like essence, since what we truly want is the good.","q":"[2] Second, [Alcibiades] expresses wonder at [Socrates'] essence when he says, 'what do you want?' [104D]: for what we want is good."},{"s":523,"ri":1,"ph":"always anxious to be present wherever I might be","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":183,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus reads this as wonder at Socrates' power, since being present everywhere and always belongs to a sleepless, unwearied power, like that of Athena.","q":"Now this belongs to a power that is sleepless and unwearied, which is also why Athene is called 'Unwearying' ( Atrutônê )."},{"s":523,"ri":1,"ph":"pestering me","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":184,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus says 'you crowd in on me' marks Socrates' unceasing activity and means 'you obstruct me', unlike the earlier 'in a mob' used of the crude lovers.","q":"but here, he uses the words 'you crowd', meaning 'you obstruct me ( aporein )', which derives from the metaphor of people walking in a crowd and being at a loss about how to move forward"},{"s":523,"ri":2,"ph":"I presume I am speaking to someone who will stay and listen","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":200,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus says the sentence should be read as a question, as Alcibiades' reply 'by all means' shows.","q":"This was intended as a question, and it ought to be read this way [sc. interrogatively]. The reply to him makes this clear"},{"s":524,"ri":4,"ph":"just as reluctant to stop speaking as I was to start","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":188,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus finds the marks of the inspired lover here: difficulty in starting shows judgement, and difficulty in stopping shows compassion and longing for the beloved's good.","q":"For the [phrase] 'diffi cult to start' indicates judgement (since he knew that Alcibiades was unprepared [before now]), whereas the [phrase] 'diffi cult to stop' indicates compassion, and [Socrates'] longing for more of his beloved."},{"s":524,"ri":4,"ph":"it would be no surprise if I were to prove just as reluctant to stop speaking","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus compares the divine, which begins and stops its gifts gradually according to the recipients' fitness, as with the long fortunes of the successful.","q":"In fact, this is just what the divine is like: it begins gradually and stops gradually, furnishing abundant goods for human beings [with a view] to the recipients' suitability ( epitêdeiotês )."},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"it is difficult for a lover to approach a man who does not give way","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":188,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus approves 'difficult' rather than 'easy' or 'impossible': it was not easy, since Alcibiades could not divert Socrates as he scorned his other lovers, and for the same reason not impossible.","q":"and he is right to use the word ‘diffi cult’ instead of ‘easy’ or ‘impossible’. For it was not easy, since it was not feasible for [Alcibiades] to divert Socrates in the same way as he scorns his other lovers; nor, for the same reason, was it impossible."},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"if some god were to say","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":194,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus gives three reasons for having a god ask: so Alcibiades cannot deny his words, from Socrates' love of tragic staging, and because the lover seeks union like the divine.","q":"Well, then, he made a god pose the questions for three reasons: [1] first, in order that the young man might not deny his own words"},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"would you prefer to live on, possessing what you now possess","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus derives from this question of the god the doctrine that lives come about by choice and not by necessity, a doctrine he also finds in the Republic.","q":"Would you rather live with what you now possess: From this one can derive the doctrine, which we have also stated, that lives are according to choice, and not according to necessity. And this is clear in the Republic as well."},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"I must nevertheless dare to speak my mind","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus approves calling the procession to the secondary 'daring', as the Pythagoreans called the Dyad daring for first separating from the Monad.","q":"He speaks well when he calls the procession [from the primary] to the secondary 'daring' ( tolman ): the Pythagoreans likewise called the Dyad 'daring', as first having dared to separate itself from the Monad."},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"I would have given up the love long ago","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus reports Harpocration's argument that these words show Socrates to be an inspired lover, since a crude lover cannot set aside his passion at will.","q":"It is clear that he is a divinely inspired lover because he says here, 'I would have put aside [my love] long ago', but the crude lover does not set aside [his love] whenever he wishes"},{"s":525,"ri":6,"ph":"I shall point out to you different notions of yours","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus says 'your own' shows Socrates knows what belongs to Alcibiades better than he does, since Alcibiades fights over shadows in ignorance of what he truly desires.","q":"And he added the [phrase] 'you your own', demonstrating that he knows what belongs to [Alcibiades] better than he does himself."},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"You think that if you appear before the Athenian people","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":10,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that in exposing these hopes Socrates applies the third, dialectical kind of purification, distinct from the telestic and the philosophic, which frees the soul from two-fold ignorance.","q":"Purification therefore being triple, Socrates here employs the third of these on Alcibiades: for those that labour under two-fold ignorance are benefited by this purification."},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"You think that if you appear before the Athenian people","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":10,"ref":"","n":"Proclus compares the purification contained in these words, which precedes all Socrates' arguments, to the telestic purifications performed before the rites of the mysteries.","q":"The purification therefore which precedes all the arguments of Socrates is comprehended in these words; and it may be said to resemble the telestic purifications which take place previous to the operations of the mysteries"},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"something which will happen in just a few days","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":191,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' explanation that Alcibiades was about to be enrolled among the ephebes, before which no one could advise the Athenians.","q":"in ten days, as Proclus relates, [Alcibiades] was to be enrolled among the Ephebes ( ephêboi ), and it was the custom that no one could give advice to the Athenians before being enrolled among them."},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"I think you would choose to die","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":196,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus explains that Socrates answers for Alcibiades because the gap between the god and Alcibiades was wide and would have been empty without a mediator.","q":"And he made himself answer for Alcibiades, since the interval ( meson ) between god and Alcibiades was wide, and it would have been empty ( kenon ), had Socrates not placed himself in this [mediating] position."},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"I think you would choose to die","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":199,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus draws two lessons from this answer: that it is better not to exist than to exist badly, and that the body impedes the acquisition of excellence.","q":"[1] that it is more profitable not to exist than to exist badly; for he says, 'You, I think, would choose to die rather than live like this.'"},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"you are worthy of more honour than Pericles","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":199,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus draws the lesson that one should advise for the benefit of the hearers, not one's own reputation, as Alcibiades intends.","q":"[3] And the third lesson is that you should not give advice ( sumbouleuein ) to your advisees with a view to your own reputation ( timê ), but instead, with a view to benefiting those who listen to you."},{"s":526,"ri":6,"ph":"if you are the greatest man here you will be greatest among the Greeks","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":201,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus observes that Socrates makes his speech climb like a ladder, from Athens to the Greeks, Europe, foreigners and the whole earth.","q":"[Socrates] has his speech ( logos ) proceed like a ladder"},{"s":527,"ri":6,"ph":"fill the whole world with your name and your power","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":13,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates leads each person up through his own desire: to the lover of dominion he shows where true power and a ruling life free from all subjection are found.","q":"But to the lover of dominion he showed where that which is powerful and governs is to be found, and what the nature is of the ruling form of life which is free from all subjection"},{"s":527,"ri":6,"ph":"apart from Cyrus and Xerxes","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":199,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus points out that the examples are dissimilar: Cyrus was called 'father' for his gentleness, Xerxes 'despot' for his violent temper.","q":"[5] Fift h, that the examples ( paradeigmata ) which he introduced were dissimilar ( anomoia ) [from one another], namely, Cyrus and Xerxes: for Cyrus was called 'father' on account of his gentle character and tolerance toward his subjects"},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"I know very well that this is the hope you entertain","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":12,"ref":"","n":"Proclus infers from Socrates' certainty that a true teacher must know exactly the aptitudes of his pupil and direct his teaching accordingly, since not everyone is to be trained alike.","q":"For it is fit that he who instructs should accurately know the aptitudes of those that are instructed, and conformably to these should direct his attention; since every one is not to be disciplined in a similar manner."},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"I am not guessing","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":208,"ref":"Lecture 5","n":"Olympiodorus notes that 'I know well' is fittingly placed after the demonstrations, and compares Diomedes' words to Athena, here spoken from the god's side.","q":"It was fitting that he placed the [phrase] 'I know well' after the demonstrations ( apodeixeis ), as he did at the beginning too [104C]."},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"dear son of Clinias and Deinomache","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":236,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus defends naming both parents as neither untimely nor excessive: given noble birth on both sides, it implies no relative can give the power Socrates can.","q":"Rather, considering the young man's noble birth on both sides, it is appropriate to use these words to express the point that 'neither your relatives on your mother's side, nor those on your father's side, can offer you the power that I can provide'."},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"for without me it is impossible to bring all these plans of yours to completion","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":221,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus' third answer to the charge of boasting: read precisely, the words are modest, for 'without' is a material preposition, suited to matter, and Socrates also adds 'with the god'.","q":"For he says, ‘with the god’s support, of course’ [105E], and ‘It is impossible to put any of these plans ( dianoêmata ) of yours into effect without me’ [105D]. (The word ‘without’ is a material ( hulikos ) preposition, and suitable to matter ( hulê ), since ‘without’ matter there is nothing here [sc. in the perceptible world] to think about)."},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"such power do I believe I have over you and your affairs","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":237,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus glosses 'your affairs' as Alcibiades' activities and 'you yourself' as his soul, the strict sense of 'me' in the Apology.","q":"He says 'for your practical affairs' meaning 'for your activities ( energeiai )', and 'for you yourself ( autos )' meaning 'for your soul'"},{"s":528,"ri":6,"ph":"I think that is why the god would not allow me to converse with you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":237,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus reasons that if the god formerly turned Socrates away to avoid speaking in vain, his not doing so now is not in vain either.","q":"For if the good man ( spoudaios ) does nothing in vain, and neither does nature ( phusis ), then the god must [do nothing in vain] to an even greater degree."},{"s":529,"ri":6,"ph":"except me, with the help of the god, of course","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":237,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus takes the qualification as a sign that Socrates is not boasting, since he claims the ability not for himself alone but with the god.","q":"Notice, again, a sign that Socrates is not boasting; for he did not only say that he himself was able, but 'with the god'."},{"s":529,"ri":6,"ph":"the power you desire","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":229,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the power Socrates offers is knowledge, which is the most powerful thing in the soul and cannot be taken away by a tyrant.","q":"he means that he will provide knowledge ( epistêmê ) to him, and knowledge is a kind of power"},{"s":530,"ri":7,"ph":"you seem even stranger to me now since you began to speak","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":237,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus glosses 'strange' as more paradoxical and wondrous, noting that we too wonder more at the divine when visibly illuminated than when it is silent.","q":"And it is appropriate that Socrates seems more wondrous to Alcibiades now, after he has conversed with him, than when he was silent."},{"s":530,"ri":7,"ph":"if I really do have these intentions","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":238,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus says the gifted Alcibiades neither grants nor denies but takes a middle ground by a hypothetical, since a democratic speaker must not appear eager for power.","q":"He does not grant [Socrates' claims], because in a democracy it was customary for the speaker not to appear keen on power, and so he shrank from this"},{"s":530,"ri":7,"ph":"how shall I attain them through you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":239,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus reports Iamblichus' puzzle and solution: 'through you' makes Socrates an instrument, but perfected souls govern with the god, so Socrates rightly says 'with the god'.","q":"Thus Socrates, with reference to his own, more fulfilled capacities ( dunameis ), said ' with the god'; but Alcibiades was referring to his less complete [capacities], and so he said ' through you'"},{"s":531,"ri":8,"ph":"So are you asking if I can deliver a long speech","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":239,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates forestalls Alcibiades, used to oratory, from expecting long speeches, and implants the notion that he will converse dialectically.","q":"By this [remark], Socrates aims to prepare Alcibiades not to expect long speeches from him."},{"s":531,"ri":8,"ph":"only one small service","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":239,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus says 'brief' fits a philosophy needing no hypotheses, and 'service' shows the would-be ruler of all as Socrates' servant, answering by his own self-moved activity.","q":"and he calls this 'doing a service', in order that the person who desires rule over all should be shown, in this case, to be Socrates' servant."},{"s":531,"ri":10,"ph":"Does it seem difficult to answer questions?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":240,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus says answering is not onerous with Socrates, a midwife seeking the young man's good rather than an eristic hunting mistakes; questioning is the harder task.","q":"but since it is Socrates, the 'midwife' who aims to benefit youths and set them straight, it is suitable [to say] 'not onerous'. On the contrary, it is the [work of] questioning that is onerous"},{"s":531,"ri":12,"ph":"So answer then","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":233,"ref":"Lecture 6","n":"Olympiodorus gives reasons for requiring answers, among them that the lover wishes to embrace his beloved, and dialogue by brief exchanges is like an embrace.","q":"Alternately, [we reply] that Socrates, being a lover, wants to embrace ( periplokê ) his beloved; and the dialogue form is similar to an embrace"},{"s":532,"ri":16,"ph":"when you were about to ascend the platform","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":296,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates uses rhetorical language for an aspiring orator, setting up a podium and audience and casting himself as the bit to restrain the young man.","q":"He furnishes [Alcibiades] with an audience and a theatre, you see, and lays out the podium ( bêma ) with [Alcibiades] rushing toward it, and he adds himself as the bit [in his mouth]"},{"s":532,"ri":16,"ph":"Is it about issues which you know better than they do?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":296,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the first premiss: the good adviser knows his subject better than his hearers, since equal knowledge is useless and inferior knowledge madness.","q":"Here is the first premiss, that 'The good adviser knows the subject of his advice better than those whom he is about to advise: since his knowledge isn't worse, for that would be crazy; nor is it equal, for that would be useless.'"},{"s":533,"ri":20,"ph":"what you have learned from others or discovered yourself","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":17,"ref":"","n":"Proclus treats learning and discovery as the two paths of knowledge proper to human souls: higher beings need neither, and irrational natures are capable of neither.","q":"Plato considers Mathesis and Invention as paths of knowledge adapted to our souls."},{"s":533,"ri":20,"ph":"learned from others or discovered yourself","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":17,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives both paths from Hermes: discovery from Hermes as son of Maia, in whom Inquiry secretly resides, and learning from Hermes as messenger of Zeus.","q":"For both, indeed, are derived from the Mercurial order; but Invention so far as Mercury is the son of Maia the daughter of Atlas; and Mathesis, so far as he is the messenger of Jupiter."},{"s":533,"ri":20,"ph":"discovered yourself","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":17,"ref":"","n":"Proclus ranks discovery between two kinds of learning: learning from higher natures surpasses it, learning from fellow men falls below it, and discovery best suits the self-moving soul.","q":"But when Mathesis proceeds supernally to souls from more excellent natures, it is better than Invention; but when from co-ordinate natures, as from men exciting our gnostic power, it is then subordinate to Invention."},{"s":533,"ri":20,"ph":"Don't you only know what you have learned from others or discovered yourself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":296,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the second premiss, that whoever knows better either learned or discovered it, since knowledge is twofold, moved by another or by oneself.","q":"[Here is] the second premiss, that 'The one who knows better either learned or discovered [his knowledge]': for understanding is twofold, as we have learned."},{"s":533,"ri":24,"ph":"Did you ever want to investigate or learn anything you thought you knew?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":20,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that only one in simple ignorance investigates: the wise man is already full of knowledge, and the doubly ignorant man lacks even the starting points of inquiry.","q":"Thus therefore the man of science, and the ignorant in a two-fold respect, do not investigate any thing; the one in consequence of being wise, and as it were full of knowledge; and the other in consequence of not even assuming the principles of investigation, through the falsehood with which he is surrounded."},{"s":533,"ri":24,"ph":"Did you ever want to investigate or learn anything you thought you knew?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":296,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the third premiss, distinguishing investigation and instruction as the means from discovery and learning as the ends.","q":"[Here is] the third premiss, that 'The person who has learned or discovered something, either investigated it or was taught: the latter are the means, the former the ends.'"},{"s":534,"ri":26,"ph":"was there a time when you did not think you knew","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":21,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reports the objection, raised by some, that this seems to contradict the Phaedo, where Socrates says we cannot name the time we received our knowledge of the Forms.","q":"But Socrates, when he admits that there was a time in which we did not think ourselves knowing in what we now know, is thought by some to contradict what he says in the Phædo"},{"s":534,"ri":26,"ph":"was there a time when you did not think you knew","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":22,"ref":"","n":"Proclus answers that knowledge is two-fold: essential knowledge of the Forms has no prior time, but the distinct, actualised knowledge of them is acquired at a time we can name.","q":"Of the conception therefore of forms subsisting in us essentially, there is no preceding time; for we perpetually possess it; but we can relate the time in which we acquired a knowledge of forms according to energy and a distinct subsistence."},{"s":534,"ri":26,"ph":"So was there a time when you did not think you knew","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":297,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the final premiss and recasts the argument as a first-figure prosyllogism and a second-figure syllogism concluding that Alcibiades is not a good adviser.","q":"We should understand that the prosyllogism is in the first figure, the syllogism in the second figure."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"you learned writing, lyre-playing and wrestling","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":24,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says these Athenian subjects were images of the guardians' disciplines: letters pointing to dialectic, the lyre to music, wrestling to gymnastic, the education of a well-born youth.","q":"grammar having a reference to dialectic discipline; playing on the harp pertaining to music, and wrestling to gymnastic; in which those youths that were well-born were instructed."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"writing, lyre-playing and wrestling","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":24,"ref":"","n":"Proclus calls all three Mercurial disciplines, noting that statues of Hermes stood in the wrestling-schools, that he is honoured as the lyrist, and that letters and reasoning are his invention.","q":"And all these are Mercurial disciplines. For this God is the inspective guardian of gymnastic exercises; and hence hermæ, or carved statues of Mercury, were placed in the Palæstræ"},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"writing, lyre-playing and wrestling","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":25,"ref":"","n":"Proclus links these studies to the planetary ages of life: the second age, ruled by Hermes, is when we take up letters, music and wrestling.","q":"But our second age participates of Mercurial prerogatives; because we then apply ourselves to letters, music, and wrestling."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"you certainly had no desire to learn to play the flute","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":26,"ref":"","n":"Proclus rejects the explanation that vain Alcibiades avoided the flute because it disfigures the face, preferring that well-ordered states, and Plato, exclude wind-instruments.","q":"It has then been said by some, that being vain of his person from his youth, he avoided that deformity of the face which is occasioned by blowing the pipe or flute. But it is better to say that well-instituted polities are averse to the art of playing on wind-instruments; and therefore neither does Plato admit it."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"no desire to learn to play the flute","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":26,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes calming from stirring instruments: education uses the calming kind, while the stirring pipe belongs to the mysteries, where it rouses reason towards the divine.","q":"But the motive instruments are adapted to enthusiastic energy: and hence, in the mysteries and mystic sacrifices, the pipe is useful; for the motive power of it is employed for the purpose of exciting the dianoëtic power to a divine nature."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"I know fairly well what it is that you have learned","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":297,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus approves the qualification 'just about', joking that Alcibiades might know of some knick-knack in his house unknown to Socrates.","q":"He was right to add 'just about' ( skhedon ti ): for it's likely that Alcibiades was aware of some knick- knack ( skeuarion ) in his house that Socrates didn't know about!"},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"you learned writing, lyre-playing and wrestling","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":292,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus explains these three studies as ordering the tripartite soul: letters train reason, the lyre tames spirit, and wrestling strengthens desire.","q":"the reason ( logos ) was organised by means of reading and writing, spirited emotion ( thumos ) tamed by means of playing the cithara, and desire ( epithumia ) strengthened and made tough by wrestling."},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"you certainly had no desire to learn to play the flute","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":292,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the flute was not studied because it belongs to ecstasy rather than education and prevents the use of speech, which is why Athena threw it away.","q":"first, because this [instrument] has to do with ecstasy and has more to do with inspiration and not with education. For in cithara-playing, it is possible to use reasoned speech ( logos ) as well, but in flute-playing that is not at all the case"},{"s":534,"ri":28,"ph":"unless you have somehow been learning something without my noticing","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":297,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus compares Diomedes' words to Athena and likens Socrates, always present with Alcibiades, to the conscience that attends all our actions.","q":"For Socrates was always present with Alcibiades, like our conscience, which attends each of our actions."},{"s":535,"ri":30,"ph":"will you stand forth as adviser to the Athenians when they are deliberating about writing","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":287,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus notes that the assembly deliberates not about how to write or play but whether to admit teachers of these arts, as Achilles advised using a prophet.","q":"But they don't hold assemblies about these matters or debate them – or if they do, not about how one should play the cithara or wrestle, but about whether they should welcome ( paralambanein ) teachers of gymnastics or of the cithara or of reading and writing in their city."},{"s":535,"ri":36,"ph":"presumably not about house-building","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":297,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus says house-building is excluded not for its low value but because Alcibiades knows he is ignorant of it; the prophecy example follows to show status is irrelevant.","q":"We should understand that it is not because advice about house-building has a low value that Socrates said 'Then you will not give advice when they deliberate about house-building', but rather because Alcibiades is ignorant about house-building."},{"s":536,"ri":40,"ph":"Nor indeed when they are deliberating about a prophecy","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":297,"ref":"Lecture 7","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes two kinds of prophecy in Plato: inspired madness, which is unteachable, and the investigative skill meant here, on which the prophet advises better.","q":"The prophetic skill is twofold in Plato, one kind being a kind of madness, as he says in the Phaedrus , and this is inspired and divine; the other is, so to speak, 'investigative' and 'searching', which is indeed a 'skill'"},{"s":537,"ri":50,"ph":"Whenever they are considering their own affairs, Socrates","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":303,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades, seeing the division is inescapable, answers from outside it and without defining his terms.","q":"and after he has realised that the division is inescapable, he grants it, but he gives an answer from outside [the division] when he says, 'When they deliberate concerning their own affairs.'"},{"s":537,"ri":50,"ph":"their own affairs","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":304,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus remarks that had Alcibiades meant 'their own' as their souls and 'affairs' as their activities, in the philosophers' sense, the answer would have been good.","q":"Now if he had said 'their own' according to philosophers' principles, referring to their souls and their real essences ( ousiai ), and 'affairs' referring to their activities ( energeiai ), then his answer would have been a good one"},{"s":537,"ri":51,"ph":"issues related to shipbuilding and what sort of ships they should get built","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":304,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus judges the example well chosen: a statesman does not build ships but may advise on them, as Themistocles did when he interpreted the oracle.","q":"And his choice of example was a good one: of course shipbuilding is not appropriate to the statesman, but advising [the state] about shipbuilding is"},{"s":537,"ri":53,"ph":"because you do not know how to build ships","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":319,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades denies advising on shipbuilding not because the example misses but because of his own ignorance, as his agreement here shows.","q":"And we should recognise that it is not because the example is off the mark that he denies that he offers such advice, but because of [his own] ignorance."},{"s":538,"ri":56,"ph":"When they are considering war, Socrates, or peace","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":306,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus offers three reasons for naming war and peace: Alcibiades loves conflict, he is a natural general, or the Athenians were then often deliberating about war.","q":"[1] He may have said 'concerning war and peace' because he is a person who enjoys conflict ( philoneikos ), someone who lives according to the conflict-loving way of life."},{"s":538,"ri":56,"ph":"When they are considering war, Socrates, or peace","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":308,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus faults the answer for starting from war, which is inferior and contrary to nature, rather than from peace; hence Socrates corrects him by putting peace first.","q":"And third – again speaking from the standpoint of current affairs – he did not begin from the [condition] that is superior and natural, namely peace, but from war, which is inferior and contrary to nature."},{"s":538,"ri":56,"ph":"or some other aspect of civic affairs","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":319,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus notes that this covers the other four subjects of political advice, while war is named first because our first words disclose the life we live.","q":"Well, we say this: we utter our first words according to the kind of life that we live; for our first words disclose which kind of life belongs to us, and which things give us pleasure."},{"s":538,"ri":57,"ph":"with whom they should make peace, and against whom they should wage war","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":319,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates puts peace, the natural condition, first, and takes up all the circumstances: against whom, by what means, where and when.","q":"We should understand that in setting the young man straight, Socrates, as we have already commented, prioritises what's natural, by saying 'With whom they should make peace' [first, before mentioning war]."},{"s":539,"ri":65,"ph":"if the Athenians were deciding with whom they should wrestle or spar","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":314,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus says the examples are clear, since Alcibiades was trained in them, and relevant, wrestling being a small war and music suited to peace.","q":"these examples are clear, since Alcibiades already had an education in them, and they are relevant, since wrestling is a 'small war', while music is also itself suitable to peace, since it is pleasing to both sides."},{"s":541,"ri":80,"ph":"I do not understand","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":320,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus rejects the view that Alcibiades was unsure whether a general or specific goal was asked, holding that he had learned song, melody and rhythm as three separate things.","q":"So the right solution is that Socrates understood the three species [of music] in a unified way, and questioned the youth about a single thing, but Alcibiades, who had learned these species as three different things, was uncertain about what kind of answer to give"},{"s":541,"ri":81,"ph":"whatever happens in accord with a skill is correct","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":320,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus observes that 'correct' is not the most beautiful: a painter of Thersites paints correctly by the rules of his art even though his subject is ugly.","q":"For 'what's done by the rules of the skill' is not the same as the most beautiful thing: even when the subject is base and ugly, if it comes about according to the skill's rules, it's 'correct'."},{"s":542,"ri":89,"ph":"What is the collective skill called?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":321,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes three senses of whole in Plato, before the parts, in the part, and among the parts, and says Socrates here means the whole before the parts and in the part.","q":"Here, then, in the case of the musical skill, Socrates speaks [of the whole] before the parts, and what is in the part as a whole, when he uses the words 'as a whole'."},{"s":542,"ri":91,"ph":"To which goddesses does the skill belong?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":321,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus calls this midwifery: by recalling the Muses, and through them Memory, Socrates stirs memory in Alcibiades so he can name the goal of the musical skill himself.","q":"he leads him to remember the Muses, and through them Memory, so that, on this basis, he might stir up the memory in the young man's soul"},{"s":543,"ri":96,"ph":"Musically, I suppose","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":316,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus divides musical performance into song, rhythm and melody, each with its own goal: metrical, rhythmical and harmonious performance.","q":"(And it should be understood that 'musical' performance falls into three parts, namely, song ( ôidêi ), rhythm ( rhuthmôi ), and melody ( melei )."},{"s":544,"ri":97,"ph":"So come on, what do you call the better","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus says Plato puts the imperative ‘come’ here because it suits a soul that grasps things step by step, discursively, rather than all at once as intellect does.","q":"Plato has used the [imperative verb] ‘come’ because it is appropriate to a soul that understands facts in sequence ( metabatikôs ), and not at one grasp, as intellect ( nous ) does."},{"s":544,"ri":98,"ph":"But I am not really able to say.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus hears in ‘really can’t’ a young man sinking and blushing with shame at being at a loss on the very matters on which he claimed to be a counsellor.","q":"This is clear from the phrase ‘really can’t’, which is uttered by the young man out of shame at being at a loss."},{"s":545,"ri":99,"ph":"it would be somewhat shameful","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates, wary of the ill-will a critique can cause, rebukes indirectly, setting beside it a field (diet) where Alcibiades does know, to soften the sharpness of the refutation.","q":"he does not come to his rebuke in a direct or obvious way, but by juxtaposing a different field where the young man does have knowledge, and using this to soft en the sharpness of his critique."},{"s":545,"ri":99,"ph":"it would be somewhat shameful","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus thinks ‘shameful’ well chosen, since shame is the very contrary of what suits a youth fond of his reputation, who vaunts himself on his beauty and dazzling appearance.","q":"And he was right to use the word ‘shameful’; for shame is the contrary to someone who is fond of his reputation and vaunts himself for his beauty and dazzling appearance."},{"s":545,"ri":99,"ph":"saying one is better than the other at this time and to this extent","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus reads the three qualifications as circumstances of diet: ‘better than another’ is the kind of food, ‘now’ its proper time, and ‘to this extent’ the quantity, citing Hippocrates that hunger is the mother of health.","q":"He refers to the circumstances: by saying ‘better than another’, he means the kind of food, whether it is cooked rather than raw, whether it goes down easily and is easy to digest or the contrary;"},{"s":545,"ri":103,"ph":"what we call this as we proceed","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":340,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates turns to something plainer: the accusation names the unjust agent, a composite participant, while ‘what we call it’ names injustice itself, the simpler thing participated, which therefore comes last.","q":"And the unjust actor participates, and hence is composite, but injustice is participated, and hence is simple. For that reason too, he puts [injustice] at the end, when he said ‘And what do we call it?’, i.e. ‘injustice’:"},{"s":546,"ri":104,"ph":"we claim to have been deceived or suffered violence or deprivation","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":334,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus faults Alcibiades for listing being tricked, attacked and deprived as three distinct things, when the one common name ‘injustice’ covers them all, each being a case of being wronged.","q":"Third, [Alcibiades] erred when he introduced as three distinct things ‘being tricked’, ‘being attacked’ and ‘being deprived of property’, although it is possible to use the common name ‘injustice’ for each of them."},{"s":546,"ri":104,"ph":"we claim to have been deceived or suffered violence or deprivation","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":327,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus objects that trickery, force and taking are not unjust in themselves but only conditionally, since they can serve a good end, as in refusing to return a deposited sword to a madman.","q":"Second, he erred because these [three actions are not unjust] unconditionally, but in a conditional way – that is, somebody could put all of these means to a good end."},{"s":546,"ri":104,"ph":"we claim to have been deceived or suffered violence or deprivation","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":335,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus also defends the answer: deceit, violence and deprivation touch reason, spirit and appetite, the whole tripartite soul, and are named in the right order, as reason rules spirit and spirit appetite.","q":"The first is that he touched on the entire tripartition of our soul in the course of his answer:"},{"s":546,"ri":105,"ph":"That will do. In what way do we suffer each of these?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":342,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus notes that the imperative ‘hold on’ (ekhe) imitates epic style, and that Socrates presses only the second error: these acts are ambiguous, done either justly or unjustly.","q":"so he charges [Alcibiades] with the second error, that these things he said are not unambiguous ( hapla ), but ambiguous ( epamphoterizonta )."},{"s":546,"ri":108,"ph":"they are completely and entirely different","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":343,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes the two words Alcibiades runs together: ‘whole’ refers to something continuous, ‘every’ to something discrete, so one class can be better as a whole without every member being better.","q":"Granted that both refer to the universal ( katholou ), still, ‘whole’ refers to something continuous ( sunekhes ), and ‘every’ to something discrete ( diôrismenon )."},{"s":547,"ri":110,"ph":"he ought to wage war against those who are behaving justly, he would not admit this","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":343,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus places Alcibiades between Callicles and Thrasymachus, who both believe and practise war on the just, and Socrates, who does neither: Alcibiades believes it but would not do or admit it.","q":"And Alcibiades, as the man in the middle, maintains that he believes it, but does not do so in practice."},{"s":547,"ri":111,"ph":"this apparently is not lawful","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":343,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus glosses the pair: ‘lawful’ is what is just by convention, ‘noble’ what is just by nature, so warring on the just is improper on both counts.","q":"‘Lawful’ ( nomimos ) is what is just by convention, but ‘noble’ ( kalos ) is what is just by nature."},{"s":547,"ri":115,"ph":"does 'the better' I was asking about turn out to be nothing but the more just?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":317,"ref":"Lecture 8","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' puzzle why the statesman's goal is not named paronymously, and his answer that justice and constitution are the same thing, as in a soul and a state.","q":"Indeed, there is no difference between [the facts of] justice ( dikaiosunê ) and a constitution ( politeia ), whether small or large: for the constitution of a state is exactly what justice is in a soul."},{"s":547,"ri":115,"ph":"turn out to be nothing but the more just","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":343,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus explains the idiom: in Plato’s style the question whether the ‘better’ is ‘anything else’ stands by paraleipsis for ‘if not this’, i.e. the better in war is simply the more just.","q":"For in Plato’s style, ‘anything else’ stands by paraleipsis for ‘if not this’."},{"s":548,"ri":117,"ph":"going to a teacher who taught you to distinguish the more just and the more unjust","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":34,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that since we are born imperfect and knowledge does not come from bodily change, learning or discovery must precede knowledge, so Alcibiades is rightly asked who taught him.","q":"It remains, therefore, that either discipline or invention must precede knowledge; and Alcibiades is very properly requested to tell who was the cause of his acquiring discipline, and whence he knows what the just is, if he does know."},{"s":548,"ri":117,"ph":"Well, who is he?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":34,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why a teacher must be named: learning is a motion, every motion needs a moving cause, and the teacher is the cause of learning.","q":"For discipline is a motion; but all motion requires a moving cause. It requires therefore a teacher, for he is the cause of discipline."},{"s":548,"ri":117,"ph":"How can this be, dear Alcibiades?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":343,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘my friend’ is deliberate: Socratic refutations are medicine smeared with honey, and Socrates conciliates the youth before provoking him with proof of his ignorance.","q":"The phrase ‘my friend’ ( ô phile ) isn’t included here by accident, but because (as we have often remarked) Socratic exhortations and refutations are like medicines drenched in honey."},{"s":548,"ri":117,"ph":"Tell me too, so that you may recommend me to him as a pupil.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":344,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus asks why Socrates, who knows justice, pretends to want a teacher, and answers that a lover plays dumb along with his beloved, while as a teacher he understands.","q":"Well, we reply that it is necessary for the lover both to play dumb with ( sunagnoein ) his beloved, and to play along with him ( sunagesthai );"},{"s":548,"ri":119,"ph":"I swear this by our common friendship","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":344,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus rejects the view that the god of Friendship is Eros, since the love is not yet mutual, and identifies him as Zeus, the ruling god befitting both philosopher and ambitious youth.","q":"Well, we say that it is Zeus, since he befits both [Socrates and Alcibiades], on account of his function as a ruler ( arkhikos )."},{"s":548,"ri":119,"ph":"I would never break that oath","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus asks whether Socrates would break oaths by other gods, and answers that ‘to you’ must be understood: an oath sworn to a friend is graver to break than one sworn to strangers.","q":"Instead, we respond [to this puzzle] that we need to specify in addition ‘an oath to you ’ ( pros se ): for it is more reasonable to break an oath made to unfamiliar people or to strangers, than to friends."},{"s":548,"ri":119,"ph":"I would never break that oath","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"As an alternative, Olympiodorus suggests perjury is graver by gods with a particular sphere, here the epithet Friendship, as Plato forbids perjury by Zeus of Strangers or of Kinship.","q":"Alternatively, it might be that it is more serious to swear falsely by gods who have a particular sphere [of responsibility] than by the others."},{"s":549,"ri":123,"ph":"Yes, indeed, if you were to search for it.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus takes Socrates’ emphatic ‘of course’ as more than a compliment: it shows his trust in Alcibiades’ natural gifts, which would certainly have found justice had he searched.","q":"The phrase ‘of course’ is not merely [complimentary] here, but shows that Socrates trusts Alcibiades’ natural gift s ( euphuïa ):"},{"s":550,"ri":127,"ph":"able to say when it was that you did not know the just and the unjust","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":37,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says inquiry begins from simple ignorance, so the time when one suspected one's ignorance must be identifiable; hence Socrates asks Alcibiades for that time.","q":"This being the case, it is necessary that the time should be known in which we suspected that we did not know: and hence Socrates desires Alcibiades to tell him the time in which he suspected his want of knowledge."},{"s":550,"ri":127,"ph":"So are you able to say when it was","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":37,"ref":"","n":"Proclus qualifies the role of time: the time of the bodily cosmos measures the soul's embodied life, while another, incorporeal time measures the soul's separate activities.","q":"For time is two-fold; one kind being that which is consubsistent with the natural life and corporeal motion of the universe, and an other kind that which pervades through the life of an incorporeal nature."},{"s":550,"ri":127,"ph":"let your answer be the truth so that our discussions may not be conducted in vain","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus explains that Socrates now asks for truthfulness because the premisses are particular, drawn from Alcibiades’ own history; he finds here the Platonic doctrine that nothing follows necessarily from false premisses.","q":"But as it is here, since the premises are particular and drawn from personal history, he asks him to tell the truth, since if he answers falsely, the argument will proceed in vain."},{"s":550,"ri":131,"ph":"And before that you were a child","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes ‘little boy’ (to seven) from ‘boy’ (seven to fourteen), after which one is an adolescent, so Socrates rightly calls Alcibiades a boy five years earlier.","q":"one is a ‘little boy’ up to the age of seven, then a ‘boy’ from one’s seventh year until the fourteenth year – then one becomes an adolescent ( ephêbos ) in and after that year."},{"s":551,"ri":135,"ph":"when you were playing dice or some other game","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":39,"ref":"Lecture 2","n":"Olympiodorus cites the boy's cry of being cheated at dice as proof that Alcibiades never thought himself ignorant of justice, and so never sought a teacher.","q":"even when he was a child and very young, when playing dice he used to say to the others, swearing an oath, that 'I'm being cheated!' ( adikoumai ), as though he knew exactly what was just ( dikaios )"},{"s":551,"ri":135,"ph":"that he was bad and unfair and that he was cheating","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":345,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus sees in the boy’s confident accusations a sign of a noble nature, grasping injustice from the innate common concept alone, before any teacher could have taught him.","q":"It is a sign of the youth’s magnificent and noble nature, that from boyhood he considered cheaters to be villains, and grasped this from his [innate] common concept ( apo tês koinês ennoias ) alone."},{"s":552,"ri":140,"ph":"I thought I knew and I did actually know.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":338,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades’ false conviction: he was beguiled by the universal formulas implanted in his soul, like those rich in dreams who wake to find their hands empty.","q":"Now, we reply to the first puzzle that he imagined he knew justice without having that knowledge because he was beguiled by the universal formulas ( logoi ) implanted in his soul"},{"s":552,"ri":143,"ph":"Well, when was it that you thought you did not know?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":339,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades cannot name the time because Socrates means actual knowledge, while Alcibiades has always possessed latent knowledge in his soul and so cannot date his ignorance.","q":"As for the second puzzle, we should reply that he is at a loss because it was with a view to actual knowledge that Socrates asked him to state the time when he did not imagine himself to know,"},{"s":552,"ri":144,"ph":"Yes, by Zeus, Socrates, I really cannot say.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":39,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reconciles the two speakers: Socrates means actualised knowledge, which time precedes, while Alcibiades has essential knowledge, always possessed, whose beginning cannot be dated.","q":"So that, if Socrates speaks about one science, and Alcibiades about another, both these assertions are true, viz. that time precedes science, and that the time prior to its presence cannot be told"},{"s":553,"ri":147,"ph":"if you neither discovered this nor learned it","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":336,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus names learning and discovery as the two routes to knowledge: we discover as self-moved agents and learn when moved by another; gifted souls tend more to discovery.","q":"We ‘discover’ something as self-moved actors ( autokinêtôs energountes ), but we ‘learn’ something when we are moved by another ( heterokinêtôs )."},{"s":553,"ri":147,"ph":"how do you know it and from what source?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":346,"ref":"Lecture 9","n":"Olympiodorus reads the two questions as the two routes: ‘how’ implies he did not search and discover it, ‘where’ that he did not study with anyone and learn it.","q":"He says ‘how’, meaning ‘not by having looked into it and found it out’, and ‘where’, meaning ‘not by having studied with anyone else and learned it’."},{"s":553,"ri":148,"ph":"But perhaps I did not give you the right answer","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":357,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades, with natural quickness, abandons the refuted premiss that he discovered justice and claims instead to have learned it.","q":"Here, as we have pointed out, [Alcibiades] rejects one of the premisses, suggesting that he gave a poor answer when he said that he knew about justice by finding out about it."},{"s":553,"ri":150,"ph":"I think that I learned in the same way that everyone else learns.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":42,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that, shaken from discovery, Alcibiades falls back on the lower kind of learning and, lacking a scientific teacher of justice, flees to the multitude as his guide.","q":"he now again comes to the second kind of discipline, and, being dubious with respect to the truly scientific teacher of things just, flies to the multitude, and their unstable life, and considers these as the leader of the knowledge of what is just."},{"s":554,"ri":152,"ph":"From the multitude.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":42,"ref":"","n":"Proclus likens Socrates' refutation here to Hercules cutting off the Hydra's heads, showing that no multitude deserves belief about what is just and unjust.","q":"Here therefore Socrates, like another Hercules, cutting off the Hydra’s heads, shows that every multitude is unworthy to be believed respecting the knowledge of things just and unjust."},{"s":554,"ri":152,"ph":"From the multitude.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":43,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that ambitious Alcibiades hung his opinion on the multitude, so Socrates must first show that popular opinion has no authority in judging things.","q":"For, in the first place, Alcibiades, being ambitious, suspended his opinion from the multitude, and about this was filled with astonishment."},{"s":554,"ri":152,"ph":"From the multitude.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":351,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades’ appeal to the many: he thought justice, like language, a matter of convention, which the many master; but justice is not conventional, so Socrates grants one and rejects the other.","q":"because he reckoned that both are matters determined by general convention ( thesis )."},{"s":554,"ri":153,"ph":"when you have recourse to the multitude","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":43,"ref":"","n":"Proclus extends the argument inward: the multitude corresponds to the many-headed beast in each soul, our lowest and most various part, which is no judge of truth.","q":"In the third place, Socrates shows, that in each of us, as he says, there is a many-headed wild beast, which is analogous to the multitude; for this is what the people is in a city, viz. the various and material form of the soul, which is our lowest part."},{"s":554,"ri":153,"ph":"You are not resorting to serious teachers","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":53,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why the many lack knowledge: embodied life is unnatural to souls, so, as in a pestilent region, the diseased naturally outnumber the healthy.","q":"Hence, as it is not wonderful that in pestilential places the diseased should be more numerous than the healthy; in like manner we ought not to wonder that in generation souls obnoxious to passions and full of depravity abound more than those of a contrary description."},{"s":554,"ri":153,"ph":"You are not resorting to serious teachers when you have recourse to the multitude.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":364,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus links this to the Sages’ saying ‘the more, the worse’ and explains why most are bad: souls descended here fall sick, living in an alien country, as most in a plague-spot fall ill.","q":"so too in this case we ought to suppose that souls, after they descend here, fall sick rather than remaining healthy because they are dwelling in an alien country ( anoikeios ), and this is why most of them are in bad shape ( kakoi )."},{"s":554,"ri":157,"ph":"if they are unable to teach more trivial matters, could they teach more serious matters?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":352,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus raises the puzzle that ignorance of humble things need not imply ignorance of higher ones, and reports Proclus’ solution: ignorance of trivial matters more familiar to them implies ignorance of higher matters.","q":"Well, Proclus offers the following solution: he says that people who are ignorant about trivial subjects that are more familiar to them ( oikeioteros ) are also ignorant about more advanced subjects."},{"s":554,"ri":157,"ph":"if they are unable to teach more trivial matters, could they teach more serious matters?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":352,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Damascius drops Proclus’ ‘more familiar’ and solves it thus: those unable to learn more trivial things also lack knowledge of more advanced ones.","q":"Those who lack the capacity to learn about more trivial issues also lack knowledge of more advanced ones:"},{"s":555,"ri":160,"ph":"I also learned to speak Greek from them","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":365,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades singles out speaking Greek in imitation of the Pythagoreans, who admired the first name-givers as having insight into the essence of Soul, since names are set by convention.","q":"They also used to express wonder and admiration at those who first laid down names for things: for these, they explained, had insight into the essence of the Soul"},{"s":555,"ri":160,"ph":"I also learned to speak Greek from them","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":366,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes two senses of ‘speaking Greek’: simply using the Greek tongue, which the many teach, and speaking it flawlessly, which the teachers of literature teach.","q":"And note that ‘speaking Greek’ ( hellênizein ) has two meanings: it can simply mean ‘using only the Greek tongue’, which is what ‘ordinary people’ teach;"},{"s":555,"ri":161,"ph":"Yes, noble friend","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":366,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus notes that since the many lack family standing, Socrates sets the youth apart from them by addressing him as ‘noble’, well-born.","q":"Since ‘ordinary people’ have no family standing ( agenês ), Socrates sets the young man ahead of ordinary people by calling him ‘noble’ ( gennaios )."},{"s":556,"ri":167,"ph":"Shouldn’t those who know agree with one another and not differ?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":56,"ref":"","n":"Proclus answers the objection that the multitude agree in denying the gods: a vicious man is at war with himself, so he cannot truly agree with others.","q":"Every vicious man therefore is discordant with himself; and this being the case, he is much more so with others."},{"s":556,"ri":167,"ph":"those who know agree with one another and not differ","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":57,"ref":"","n":"Proclus meets the objection that philosophers disagree: dissension belongs only to the ignorant, among themselves or against the wise, never to the scientific.","q":"This doubt may be dissolved by saying that dissension is two-fold, one kind being the dissonance of the ignorant, both with themselves and with each other, and the other of the ignorant with the scientific"},{"s":556,"ri":167,"ph":"those who know agree with one another","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":58,"ref":"","n":"Proclus grounds the agreement of knowers in the fixed nature of things and shared criteria, and ultimately in the One: knowledge is an illumination of intellect, concord of the One.","q":"Science therefore is an illumination of intellect, but concord of the one: for it is a union of things different."},{"s":556,"ri":167,"ph":"Shouldn’t those who know agree with one another and not differ?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":353,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus notes that agreement does not prove knowledge, as the Democriteans agree on the void and are wrong, but by contraposition those who know agree, so disagreement shows ignorance.","q":"but those who do understand do agree with one another, as we can see by conversion by contraposition of the following premiss, that those who do not agree are ignorant."},{"s":557,"ri":173,"ph":"will they not agree on the same answer and reach for the same things","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":366,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus takes ‘agree’ to concern understanding and ‘reach for’ to concern life, and sees them joined because way of life generates beliefs and beliefs generate way of life.","q":"The verb ‘agree’ here relates to understanding ( gnôseôs esti ), and the word ‘reach’ relates to life ( zôê )."},{"s":557,"ri":173,"ph":"that is what I understand you to mean by knowing how to speak Greek","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":366,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus says this shows the first sense of speaking Greek, calling things by their right names, not the grammarians’ sense of using established names flawlessly.","q":"This doesn’t refer to the other sense of ‘speaking Greek’, namely, that with which lecturers in literature ( grammatikoi ) are concerned;"},{"s":558,"ri":181,"ph":"which of them are good at running and which are not","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":367,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes ‘runner’, a state, from ‘racer’, a fitness for racing: the many may know who runs fast, but spotting future racers belongs to the expert art of physiognomy.","q":"the former names a state ( hexis ), that of running, but the latter is the name of a fitness ( epitêdeiotês ), namely, of being fit for [competitive] racing."},{"s":559,"ri":183,"ph":"are not real teachers of these matters","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":357,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus says the word Plato uses here (krêguos, ‘helpful’) means ‘good’, as Homer’s line contrasting it with ‘bad’ news shows.","q":"He uses the word ‘helpful’ to mean ‘good’ ( agathos ), as this [verse] from the poet [Homer] makes clear in its contrast [of ‘helpful’] with ‘bad’ ( kakos ):"},{"s":560,"ri":190,"ph":"By Zeus, Socrates, not in the least.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":367,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus comments on the oath: Alcibiades says ‘yes by Zeus’ where a negative was due, but cancels its affirmative force by adding ‘far from it’, meaning ‘by no means’.","q":"but he cancelled the positive force of the ‘yes’ in ‘yes by Zeus’ nonetheless by adding ‘far from it’, which stands in for ‘by no means’ ( oudamôs ), which itself is clearly negative."},{"s":561,"ri":195,"ph":"for you have heard the Iliad and the Odyssey","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":370,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus asks how Alcibiades, taught only letters, cithara and wrestling, knows poetry, and answers that he knows those three as skills, whereas hearing poems is not learned as a skill.","q":"Well, we reply that he knows those three subjects as skills ( tekhnai )"},{"s":561,"ri":197,"ph":"Aren’t those poems about a dispute over the just and unjust?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":354,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus reads the Iliad as a pleading over justice between barbarians and Greeks: the barbarians cite Jason’s seizure of Medea, the Greeks the greater wrong of Helen’s seizure.","q":"For the Iliad is nothing other than an epic about barbarians and Greeks seeking justice ( dikaiologia ),"},{"s":561,"ri":199,"ph":"Odysseus and the suitors of Penelope too","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":354,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus reads the Odyssey as a dispute over justice: the suitors claim a widow must not refuse Ithaca’s noblest wooers, Telemachus that his father may live and they plunder his house unjustly.","q":"And in the Odyssey , the suitors and Telemachus seek justice, as the suitors maintain that a widow must not refuse the noblest men of Ithaca when they woo her with plentiful bride-gifts,"},{"s":562,"ri":201,"ph":"among whom your own father met his end","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":355,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates adds Coronea, though lesser than Troy, because it came close to home: Alcibiades’ father Clinias distinguished himself and fell there; he compares Nestor and Priam recalling Peleus to Achilles.","q":"but he described the Battle of Coronea because it came closer to home for the young man, since Alcibiades’ father Clinias distinguished himself there and lost his life"},{"s":563,"ri":203,"ph":"they disagree so intensely that they dispute with one another and inflict these misfortunes on themselves","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":354,"ref":"Lecture 10","n":"Olympiodorus explains why disputes over justice lead to the greatest wars: justice pervades our whole being, so the wronged count themselves as good as dead unless they obtain justice.","q":"it’s for this reason that people who have been treated unfairly reckon themselves as good as dead and non-existent ( anousios ) unless they can win just [compensation]: that’s why they start the greatest wars [over it]."},{"s":563,"ri":208,"ph":"from what you are saying I am not likely to","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":404,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus sees Alcibiades’ love of reputation in ‘not likely’ and ‘from what you say’: he cannot bear simply to admit his ignorance of justice.","q":"Notice how Alcibiades reveals his care for reputation ( to philotimon ), when he says ‘not very likely’ and ‘from what you say’, since he’s unable to bear simply acknowledging that he’s ignorant about justice."},{"s":563,"ri":208,"ph":"from what you are saying I am not likely to","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":405,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Using Epictetus’ stages of education, Olympiodorus says the uneducated Alcibiades blames Socrates here; later, as his education begins, he will blame himself.","q":"Likewise Alcibiades – since he lacks education – blames Socrates by saying ‘as you say, I don’t know about justice’; but later, when he begins to be educated, he will blame himself;"},{"s":564,"ri":209,"ph":"Do you also realise that you are not expressing this in the right way, Alcibiades?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":60,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that showing the answers came from Alcibiades himself serves the dialogue's aim: the soul does not receive knowledge from outside like a blank tablet.","q":"For the soul does not possess an adventitious knowledge of things, nor, like an unwritten tablet, does it externally receive the images of divine ideas."},{"s":564,"ri":211,"ph":"You are claiming that I said these things.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":64,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that making Alcibiades refuted by himself shows Socrates acting as a good daemon, who governs from within rather than acting from outside.","q":"Socrates therefore, who with respect to Alcibiades ranks in the order of a good dæmon, shows him that he is confuted by no other than himself."},{"s":564,"ri":211,"ph":"You are claiming that I said these things.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":407,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates here borrows Phaedra’s retort to her nurse, ‘you heard it from yourself, not me’, so as to say to Alcibiades ‘you said this, not I’.","q":"Socrates employs Phaedra’s saying here: for when her nurse says, ‘Do you mean Hippolytus’?, she replies ‘You heard it from yourself, not me!’"},{"s":564,"ri":217,"ph":"If I were to ask you which is greater, one or two, would you say two?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":398,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus calls these ‘interrogative’ questions, needing a stated answer rather than yes or no, so it is plainly the respondent who says something different from the questioner.","q":"but in interrogative questioning ( pusmatikôn erôtêseôn ), where the questioner says one thing, but the respondent says something different (because some explanation ( logos ) is required), <it does not escape us>;"},{"s":564,"ri":223,"ph":"Did I not ask you and did you not answer?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":63,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the fact that answerers produce everything from themselves as strong support for the doctrine that learning is recollection, the soul being an always-written tablet.","q":"Further still: this reasoning sufficiently confirms the doctrine, that disciplines are reminiscences; for this is a great argument in favour of such a dogma, that those who answer, assert all things from themselves"},{"s":565,"ri":225,"ph":"is it I, the questioner, who turns out to be speaking about these matters","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":399,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Among his proofs that the respondent speaks, Olympiodorus adds that Socrates as midwife is not the speaker, just as the midwife does not give birth but delivers others.","q":"nonetheless Socrates , in his capacity as a midwife ( hôs maieutikos ), is not the speaker, since his case is just like the midwife who does not give birth, but prepares those in labour to deliver."},{"s":565,"ri":229,"ph":"whenever there is questioning and answering, who is the speaker: the questioner or the answerer?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":61,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that being refuted by oneself is the most fitting cure, because what is one's own is felt as gentler and works more effectively than an outside rebuke.","q":"For by how much the more familiar it appears, by so much the more is the weight and pain of it diminished, and its gentleness and benefit extended; since every thing familiar or domestic is more efficacious as a remedy."},{"s":565,"ri":229,"ph":"who is the speaker: the questioner or the answerer?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":41,"ref":"Lecture 2","n":"Olympiodorus treats the principle that the answerer is the speaker as a Platonic doctrine: Socrates makes Alcibiades prove the human being is the soul, so that he becomes his own teacher.","q":"makes Alcibiades prove that the human being is the soul, so that he himself is his own teacher: here, then, is the [Platonic] doctrine ( dogma ) that the answerer is the speaker."},{"s":565,"ri":229,"ph":"whenever there is questioning and answering, who is the speaker","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":401,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus stresses the qualification: Socrates does not deny that the questioner speaks at all, only that within question and answer the respondent is the speaker.","q":"It’s not in an unqualified sense that he says ‘the questioner does not speak’, but he says that it’s ‘in the context of question and answer’ that the respondent is the [only] speaker:"},{"s":565,"ri":229,"ph":"whenever there is questioning and answering, who is the speaker","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":400,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus allows the questioner a role: he asserts no premisses but supplies the construction and the conclusion, so he is blamed if nothing follows, the respondent if falsehood follows.","q":"No: he speaks in his own right, but he doesn’t assert premisses; instead, he supplies the construction [of the syllogism] and its conclusion."},{"s":565,"ri":230,"ph":"it is the answerer, Socrates","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":62,"ref":"","n":"Proclus sees here the self-moving soul purifying itself: the answerer who is refuted applies the refutation to himself and is freed from ignorance from within.","q":"He who answers, certainly purifies himself, and is liberated from ignorance, applying confutation to himself, which accords with the self-motive nature of the soul."},{"s":566,"ri":237,"ph":"did not know about the just and the unjust but thought that he did","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":439,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus says double ignorance makes Alcibiades fall short of Soul, which knows, of Intellect, which reverts on itself, and of God, who is good, since he will harm those he advises.","q":"Now, Alcibiades fell short of three primary hypostases, Intellect ( nous ), God ( theos ), and Soul ( psukhê )."},{"s":566,"ri":237,"ph":"Alcibiades the fair, the son of Clinias","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":438,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus notes that where Homer’s Phoenix criticises Achilles through Peleus, Socrates instead makes the criticised party himself the accuser, using Alcibiades’ own words.","q":"but it doesn’t satisfy Socrates to use another persona, but instead, he actually puts the criticised party himself to use for a more effective charge,"},{"s":566,"ri":237,"ph":"Alcibiades the fair, the son of Clinias","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus takes the title ‘the fair son of Clinias’ to drive home that beauty, wealth and birth did nothing to help Alcibiades know justice.","q":"This drives home the point that ‘None [of this] helped you toward knowing about justice: not beauty, nor wealth, nor noble birth’."},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"you are blaming me without reason","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":66,"ref":"","n":"Proclus applies the Stoic grades: the doubly ignorant blame others, the progressing blame themselves; so Alcibiades first blamed Socrates, but now in simple ignorance blames himself.","q":"since, labouring under two-fold ignorance, he does not accuse himself, but Socrates: but afterwards, when he is transferred to simple ignorance, he accuses himself of dissension, and not his leader"},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"heard this from yourself and not from me","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":67,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that the ancients put severe rebukes into others' mouths; Socrates goes further, letting Alcibiades be refuted by Alcibiades, which softens the reproof.","q":"For he represents Alcibiades confuted by Alcibiades; mitigating by this method the vehemence of the reproof, and unfolding the alliance of the reprover to the reproved."},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"you are planning to embark on a mad undertaking","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":69,"ref":"","n":"Proclus defends the paradox implied by 'mad': ignorance, especially two-fold ignorance, is a prolonged madness, since like the insane the doubly ignorant know neither themselves nor others.","q":"That ignorance is a mania of an extended duration, and especially two-fold ignorance, is a paradox, but is at the same time most true."},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"So the phrase from Euripides is applicable","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus says Plato here teaches how to adapt poetry: excerpt a few phrases rather than quote whole passages, which is tedious and weakens the words.","q":"Plato teaches us how one ought to paraphrase poetic passages ( parôidein khrêseis ), namely, that one shouldn’t quote the passages themselves (since that’s tedious and deprives the words of force), but rather, [one should excerpt] some phrases:"},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"I am not the person making these statements","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus compares the questioner to the god who offers souls a choice of lives, as in the Republic, with the respondent as the soul that chooses.","q":"[He says this] because an analogy holds between the questioner and the god who offers souls their choice of life, by offering them one or the other alternative"},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"you are planning to embark on a mad undertaking","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus explains ‘mad’: Alcibiades resembles Sophocles’ Ajax slaughtering flocks as Greeks, since he thinks he knows justice and so harms those he advises, and no teacher helps one who thinks he knows.","q":"And Alcibiades is like madmen: for his case is analogous to that of Ajax in Sophocles, who supposed as he slew the flocks that they were the Greeks;"},{"s":567,"ri":239,"ph":"Yet what you are saying is actually to the point.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":442,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Democritus gives ‘you are quite right to say so’ to Socrates, Damascius to Alcibiades, and prefers Damascius, since Alcibiades is beginning to benefit and criticise himself.","q":"Democritus, surnamed 651 (because when his name is measured, it adds up to 651), takes the phrase ‘you’re quite right to say so’ as spoken by Socrates, but Damascius takes it as spoken by Alcibiades."},{"s":568,"ri":240,"ph":"I do not believe that the just and the beneficial are the same","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":71,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the question whether the just and the beneficial are the same contains all moral philosophy, since separating them puts happiness in external things.","q":"The dogma, that the just is the same with the profitable, contains the whole of moral philosophy. For those who suppose these to be different, must necessarily admit, that felicity receives its completion from externals"},{"s":568,"ri":240,"ph":"others have, I believe, performed just actions and not benefited","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":71,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that only those who define man as a soul using the body as instrument can keep the just and the beneficial identical; Epicureans, Stoics and Peripatetics cannot.","q":"But those who place the end of man in soul unindigent of corporeal possessions, and who assert that man is a soul using the body as an instrument, these admit that the just is the same with the profitable"},{"s":568,"ri":240,"ph":"the Athenians and the other Greeks seldom deliberate upon whether something is more just","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus calls this an orator’s escape: trained under Pericles, Alcibiades knows deliberative oratory aims at the advantageous, while justice belongs to the law-courts.","q":"Notice how he uses this speech to make his escape, in the spirit of orators: ‘The Athenians don’t deliberate about what’s just, but about what’s advantageous, since that’s the adviser’s goal’."},{"s":568,"ri":240,"ph":"for they regard such questions as obvious","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":442,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus thinks Alcibiades makes a good point: the Athenians treat justice as conventional and themselves as its inventors, so think it needs no teaching or advice.","q":"Alcibiades makes a good point: for since they consider just [actions] to be so by convention, and [consider themselves] to be their inventors, they don’t think it’s worthwhile to teach or give advice about them,"},{"s":568,"ri":240,"ph":"I do not believe that the just and the beneficial are the same","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":440,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates cursed those who first separated just and advantageous, since this confuses our essence, that man is soul, and our fulfilment, that virtue suffices for happiness.","q":"That’s why Socrates, too, often solemnly cursed those who first distinguished these from each other, since they had produced the worst sort of a snare for souls,"},{"s":570,"ri":243,"ph":"you decide you need to hear some new and different refutations","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":74,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the craving for novelty: souls joined to generation pursue what is new and childish and shift between opinions, whereas souls like intellect keep to the same.","q":"but when they become agglutinated to generation, they always pursue what is novel and puerile, at different times are led to different opinions"},{"s":570,"ri":243,"ph":"someone must provide you with a fresh and pristine proof","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":74,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads this garment metaphor as showing that nobler souls, holding an image of divine purity, seek outward purity, though true purity belongs to the soul's vehicle.","q":"this, which is a metaphor taken from garments, indicates, that souls of a naturally more excellent disposition possessing a conception of the immaculate purity of the gods, and carrying this about them in images, are studious of apparent purity"},{"s":570,"ri":243,"ph":"you decide you need to hear some new and different refutations","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":442,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus likens Alcibiades to a gourmand of arguments, unsatisfied with one proof and wanting the same point shown by many, as gourmands want many dishes.","q":"[He says this] because Alcibiades resembles a gourmand when it comes to arguments."},{"s":570,"ri":243,"ph":"like worn out garments which you would never wear","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":442,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘equipment’ (skeuaria) here means not implements but costume, as the words about putting them on show, and lists three Platonic senses: costume, clothing, luxury.","q":"Plato makes clear that ‘equipment’ ( skeuaria ) doesn’t mean implements ( skeuê ), as our ordinary usage has it, since he adds ‘that you would refuse to put on again’;"},{"s":570,"ri":243,"ph":"I shall pay no attention to your attacks upon the argument","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":444,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus glosses the military word ‘sally’: a sortie from a secure base; Alcibiades thought it a safe sally to ask not to be questioned again with the same arguments.","q":"The word ‘sally’ ( prodromê ) is used in cases where one side in war takes some secure base, from which he’s able to wage war from a position of security:"},{"s":571,"ri":243,"ph":"or alternatively, just go through the argument by yourself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":446,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates here admits the questioner also speaks, omitting ‘in question and answer’; he offers Alcibiades a speech at length because, once refuted, the youth would no longer answer.","q":"Notice how he is aware that the questioner, too, speaks and he never adds ‘in question and answer’, since he recognises that the respondent is the speaker in a case of question and answer."},{"s":571,"ri":244,"ph":"I do not know if I would be able to set out an argument for you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":447,"ref":"Lecture 11","n":"Olympiodorus takes Alcibiades to doubt he can sustain dialectic against Socrates, and hears in ‘facing you’ a warlike, combative tone.","q":"He means, ‘I don’t know if I have the capacity to maintain a dialectical conversation with you’: and the phrase ‘facing you’ ( pros se ) had a warlike and combative character."},{"s":571,"ri":245,"ph":"regard me as the Assembly and the populace","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":76,"ref":"","n":"Proclus calls the point that one science persuades one and many alike a hymn to science: like intellect it gives itself wholly to many without being diminished.","q":"Proclus here observes, that it is the province of the same science to persuade one person and many; which assertion is, as it were, a certain hymn and encomium of science."},{"s":571,"ri":245,"ph":"for even in the Assembly you will need to convince each one","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":485,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘each one’ shows the one is meant as part of the many, which settles the puzzle whether persuading one fool means persuading many philosophers.","q":"We reply that Plato resolves all of these questions in one phrase, when he says ‘each one by one’ ( hena hekaston )."},{"s":571,"ri":245,"ph":"regard me as the Assembly and the populace","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":486,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates brings in the Assembly, the courts and the people because the young man is inclined to oratory.","q":"And he also introduces [talk about] the Assembly and the courtroom and the people, since the young man is inclined to oratory."},{"s":572,"ri":247,"ph":"able to convince one person, both individually and in gatherings","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":484,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus infers that our fulfilment is incorporeal: unlike the body, the soul gives itself to one or many without diminution, and the teacher is even perfected by teaching.","q":"Through this syllogism, it’s made clear that our mode of fulfilment is something incorporeal, since the body is unable to give of itself to others without diminution,"},{"s":572,"ri":251,"ph":"And will that be the person who knows, namely the arithmetician?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":486,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus marks the second condition: persuading one and many requires knowing the subject, and Socrates adds ‘the one who knows’ rather than resting on mathematical necessity alone.","q":"He wasn’t satisfied with mathematical necessity as an instrument of persuasion (for the arithmetician is a kind of mathematician), but he also added ‘the one who knows ( ho eidôs ) about these things’"},{"s":573,"ri":257,"ph":"one persuades groups about the same things, while the other does it individually","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":486,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus notes the pairing ‘one by one’ and ‘in a group’ refers to the same people, taken now individually, now collectively.","q":"Notice again how he juxtaposed ‘one by one’ with ‘in a group’: it’s clear that he means the same people, at one point taken individually, at another, as a group."},{"s":573,"ri":259,"ph":"the same speaker convinces both many and one","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":76,"ref":"","n":"Proclus infers that our essence is separate from body, since bodily powers are diminished by sharing, but science remains undiminished whether imparted to one or many.","q":"For corporeal powers diminish themselves in their communications; but science, remaining one and the same, sufficient to itself and undiminished, imparts itself, in a similar manner, to one and to many."},{"s":573,"ri":261,"ph":"Then perhaps overbearingly, I am going to convince you of the opposite","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":486,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates accepts the charge of insolence: he will not only refute Alcibiades but prove the contrary true; he adds that some falsehoods are refuted not by their contrary but by an intermediate.","q":"Not only am I pushy, but the most pushy imaginable, since I don’t only want to prove your arguments false, but also to prove the contrary position true, that everything just is advantageous, and vice versa’."},{"s":573,"ri":263,"ph":"You need only answer the questions.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":486,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates insists on answers because self-refutation is the most effective kind: we are best persuaded when we refute ourselves.","q":"And [Socrates] in turn insists on his answering, for this kind of refutation ( elenkhos ) is the most efficacious of all, since we are better persuaded when we refute our own selves,"},{"s":574,"ri":269,"ph":"if you yourself do not hear from yourself that whatever is just is also beneficial","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":488,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus says the answers must come from Alcibiades himself, not poured in like water into a lifeless vessel; refutation must come from within, so that learning is recollection.","q":"rather, the refutations ( elenkhoi ) ought to be presented from one’s own self. That way, learning is recollection."},{"s":574,"ri":269,"ph":"whatever is just is also beneficial","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":481,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus cites Proclus’ rule that higher principles reach further, like stronger archers: the advantageous answers to the Good, the noble to Intellect, the just to Soul.","q":"Well, if we adopt Proclus’ rule, then loftier [beings] do not end or begin at the same point as hollower [beings]: rather, their progression extends further, like three archers of unequal power, and the stronger archers fire a great distance."},{"s":574,"ri":269,"ph":"whatever is just is also beneficial","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":483,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Iamblichus, against Proclus, holds all higher causes reach down to matter; they differ not in extent but in the more striking force of their effect.","q":"but he does make a distinction in the more ‘striking’ nature ( drimus ) of the contribution of the loftier [entities]."},{"s":574,"ri":270,"ph":"I do not think I can come to any harm","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":77,"ref":"","n":"Proclus adds that such unreasoned confidence shows nobler souls being moved by higher natures, so that they achieve good unexpectedly even without knowledge.","q":"It also indicates, that the impulses of more naturally excellent souls are excited in a certain respect by more excellent natures. Hence they accomplish much good contrary to expectation, though energizing without science."},{"s":575,"ri":271,"ph":"How prophetic you are!","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":77,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Alcibiades' confidence that no harm will come is opinion, not science, and distinguishes the merely divinatory, who opine, from true prophets, who know.","q":"For the οι μαντικοι, or the prophetic, are doxastic, and not scientific; but thole that are not only μαντικοι but, prophets, are scientific, and possess something better than human science."},{"s":575,"ri":271,"ph":"are you saying that some just actions are beneficial while others are not?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":79,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that if the just and the beneficial are the same, our good lies in virtue alone, and bodily and external goods add nothing to happiness.","q":"For, if the just is, in reality, the same with the profitable, and these are not separated from each other, our good will consist in virtue alone"},{"s":575,"ri":271,"ph":"some just actions are beneficial while others are not","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":80,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues that identifying the just with the beneficial means man is defined by the soul alone, the body being neither part of us nor completing our nature.","q":"Again: if the just is the same with the profitable, according to the assertion of Socrates, then the essence of man will be defined according to the soul herself; but the body will neither be a part of us, nor will give completion to our nature."},{"s":575,"ri":271,"ph":"How prophetic you are!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":488,"ref":"Lecture 12","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates calls Alcibiades ‘prophetic’, not ‘a prophet’: the prophet knows, while the prophetically inclined only guesses well from opinion, as the medically inclined differs from the doctor.","q":"And [Socrates] didn’t say ‘you’re a prophet ( mantis )’, since the prophet is knowledgeable , while the prophetically inclined person is unfulfilled [in the mastery of their talent]:"},{"s":575,"ri":271,"ph":"are you saying that some just actions are beneficial while others are not?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":509,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates asks not whether no just thing is advantageous but the more persuasive question, whether some are and some are not.","q":"And notice that he did not ask [Alcibiades] whether he thought that no just things were advantageous, but instead he posed the more persuasive question, whether he thought that some just thing was advantageous, but another was not."},{"s":575,"ri":274,"ph":"What are you asking me?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":509,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades’ confusion: Socrates had named two things, the just and the advantageous, so he was unsure which of them the question about noble and shameful concerned.","q":"The young man wasn’t unsure without cause, but because Socrates named two things, the just and the advatangeous, and then asked whether some of these are noble but others shameful,"},{"s":575,"ri":275,"ph":"I am asking if you ever thought that someone acted ignobly and yet justly?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":88,"ref":"","n":"Proclus supports the premise that the just is beautiful: justice gives each part of the soul its proper rank, and order and symmetry are naturally beautiful.","q":"But order and symmetry are naturally beautiful. Justice, therefore, is the source of beauty to the soul, and is itself beautiful."},{"s":575,"ri":277,"ph":"Then all that is just is also noble?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":83,"ref":"","n":"Proclus sets out the whole argument as a first-figure syllogism: everything just is beautiful, everything beautiful is good, the good is the beneficial, so everything just is beneficial.","q":"But the whole syllogism which collects that the just is profitable is as follows: Every thing just is beautiful: Every thing beautiful is good: Every thing just, therefore, is good."},{"s":575,"ri":277,"ph":"Then all that is just is also noble?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":494,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades grants the minor premiss, that everything just is noble, because as a lover of reputation he regards just deeds as praiseworthy.","q":"And the youth does not reject the minor premiss (the one stating that ‘everything just is noble’), as someone who cares about his reputation ( hôs philotimos ), because he considers just deeds praiseworthy."},{"s":575,"ri":279,"ph":"But what about noble actions?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":94,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the beautiful (to kalon) from 'calling' others to itself or from 'charming' beholders, to show that it is naturally lovable, hence desirable, hence good.","q":"Indeed, the beautiful (το καλον) is naturally lovely, whether it is so denominated δια το καλειν, because it calls others to itself, or δια το κηλειν, because it charms those that are able to behold it."},{"s":575,"ri":279,"ph":"Are they all good or are some good while others are not?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":96,"ref":"","n":"Proclus warns that the argument concerns the beauty and good in us, not first principles, where the good is beyond beauty; in the soul the good is beautiful.","q":"Let no one therefore say that the good is above beauty nor that the lovely is two-fold; for we do not now discourse concerning the first principles of things, but concerning the beautiful and the good which are in us."},{"s":575,"ri":280,"ph":"I think that some noble actions are bad","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":93,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Alcibiades rejects 'every beautiful thing is good' because he thinks beauty exists by convention, not nature, while the good is real.","q":"This was owing to his considering the beautiful to be beautiful by position, and not by nature."},{"s":575,"ri":280,"ph":"I think that some noble actions are bad","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":494,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades resists the major premiss, thinking noble opposes shameful and good opposes bad, so dying for a friend can be noble yet bad.","q":"But he resists the major premiss (the one stating that ‘everything noble is advantageous’), by arguing that some deeds are noble but bad, while other, shameful deeds are good."},{"s":576,"ri":283,"ph":"have sustained injuries or been killed","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":99,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the many wrongly think wounds and death evils: as a blunted saw does not destroy the carpenter's art, bodily harm does not reach the soul.","q":"Neither, therefore, does the carpenter, if his saw happens to be blunted, lose his art"},{"s":576,"ri":285,"ph":"Do you call such a rescue noble","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":509,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates could have argued from temperance, but because the talk concerns war he takes courage; with Charmides he would have used temperance.","q":"He could certainly have said the same things about temperance ( sophrosûnê ), but since this discussion is about warcraft, he mentioned a relevant example."},{"s":576,"ri":287,"ph":"Then the rescue is bad on account of the deaths and the injuries","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":499,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates could have answered that man is the soul, so harm to the body does not touch him, but avoids this as too philosophical and question-begging.","q":"But since these [arguments] are more philosophical (and the question would be begged right from the start, ‘On what grounds [do you claim] that the human being is the soul?’), he [instead] employs three arguments"},{"s":577,"ri":289,"ph":"is not courage one thing and death another?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":100,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Socrates' solution as the distinction of essential and accidental: a brave rescue is beautiful in itself and evil only accidentally, because death happens to follow.","q":"For a brave action is essentially beautiful, but evil, if it is so, accidentally, because it happens to him who acts bravely to die."},{"s":577,"ri":289,"ph":"is not courage one thing and death another?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":500,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus calls this the first argument: nobility attends courage and harm attends death, two qualities on two subjects; Socrates concedes death is harmful only to suit Alcibiades.","q":"For nobility attends courage on behalf of one’s friend, but harm attends death: so courage and death are not identical, at least, not unless everyone who is brave in battle dies, or everyone who dies in battle is brave."},{"s":577,"ri":291,"ph":"the rescue of friends is not noble and bad on account of the same thing","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"proclus-alcibiades","p":100,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes Socrates avoids the deeper answer, that the evil belongs to the body and not the man, since he has not yet shown our essence to be separate from body.","q":"Socrates, however, does not adopt this solution, because he has not yet demonstrated that our essence is separate from body."},{"s":577,"ri":293,"ph":"at least to the extent that it is noble","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":501,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus finds the second argument here: nobility follows courage essentially, as courage, while harm follows only accidentally, by destroying the body; what destroys courage as such is cowardice.","q":"For nobility followed courage insofar as it is courage [i.e., essentially], but harm only followed by accident, by the destruction of the underlying [body] and by allowing courage as such [to run its course]."},{"s":577,"ri":293,"ph":"which would you rather have, what is good or what is bad?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":509,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates switches to midwifery, questioning instead of teaching, so as not to overstep the god’s injunction in the Theaetetus to deliver ideas but not beget them.","q":"He changes the structure of the argument to midwifery, to avoid overstepping the injunction of the god: ‘the god made me a midwife [of ideas], but prevents me from begetting’ [ Tht. 150C]."},{"s":578,"ri":299,"ph":"At what price would you accept being deprived of this?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":505,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus sets out the midwife’s syllogism: courage is desired by Alcibiades, the desirable is good since desire grows with the good, so courage is good, and likewise the other virtues.","q":"‘Courage is desirable to Alcibiades; everything desirable is good, since desire waxes and wanes with the good (for the greater the good, the greater the desire); courage, then, is good;"},{"s":578,"ri":303,"ph":"Equal to death, so it seems.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":510,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades errs in equating cowardice with death: existing badly is worse than not existing, since courage gives well-being and life mere being; Socrates lets it pass.","q":"Notice how the young man errs here: for existing in a bad condition is worse than not existing."},{"s":580,"ri":319,"ph":"is it noble in so far as it is good","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":504,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘insofar as’ signals convertibility, so if a thing is good insofar as it is noble, it is also noble insofar as it is good.","q":"since the phrase “insofar as” implies convertibility [between two terms], it is also noble insofar as it is good."},{"s":580,"ri":321,"ph":"is not at all different from saying that it is good and yet bad","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":510,"ref":"Lecture 13","n":"Olympiodorus identifies Aristotle’s proof from two pairs of contradictories: since noble converts with good, calling a noble act harmful is implicitly calling the harmful good, a contradiction.","q":"you are not uttering a contradiction on the face of it, but you are potentially contradicting yourself, since what you’re saying is actually that the harmful is good, given that the noble is convertible with the good, and everything noble is good’."},{"s":581,"ri":325,"ph":"Then you also need to consider this.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":515,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates now proves the same conclusion again, knowing even before Aristotle that proof from particular terms is weak, this time by a universal argument.","q":"Now since he knew (even before Aristotle) that a demonstration relying on particular terms is weak, he wants to demonstrate this conclusion using a universal argument as well:"},{"s":581,"ri":325,"ph":"Does not anyone who acts nobly also act well?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":517,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Against critics who see no difference, Olympiodorus distinguishes doing noble deeds from acting well: ‘well’ is a middle term, an outflow of the good ending in the beautiful.","q":"For ‘well’ is a middle term between the good, and the noble or beautiful ( kalon ), since ‘well’ is an outflowing and a kind of radiance of the good toward the beautiful ( kalon ), beginning from the good, and ending with the beautiful."},{"s":581,"ri":327,"ph":"And those who act well are happy?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":519,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes acting well, which belongs to self-moved agents living virtuously, from happiness, a radiance from the divine granted from above to those made fit for it.","q":"for the former befits self-moved agents (since it is a distinguishing feature of self-movement to live with excellence [ kata aretên ]), but well-being has reached us from above, if we are beings who have lived in an excellent way;"},{"s":581,"ri":331,"ph":"And do they acquire this by acting well and nobly?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says Plato joins ‘well’ and ‘nobly’ deliberately, since acting well is the mean and bond flowing from the good to the beautiful.","q":"[Plato] didn’t combine these terms – ‘acting well’ and ‘acting admirably’ – at random, but because [acting] well is a mean between the [morally] beautiful and the good,"},{"s":581,"ri":335,"ph":"Is not acting well noble?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates converts one premiss here, showing how to convert the rest and so that everything advantageous is also just.","q":"Notice how he converts one premiss here, providing us with an idea ( ennoia ) of how to convert the others, and also on this account [providing us with an idea] that everything advantageous <is just>:"},{"s":582,"ri":337,"ph":"So, once again, the same thing appears to us to be both noble and good.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘once again’ is not idle: the point was proved before from particular terms and is now proved from a universal premiss.","q":"He didn’t say ‘once again’ idly, but because this had already been proven by the syllogism based on particular terms, but now has been proven using the universal premiss."},{"s":582,"ri":339,"ph":"on the basis of this argument anyway","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":530,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Following Proclus, Olympiodorus takes the qualification to mean that noble and good are the same in their subject, not absolutely, as ‘human’ and ‘capable of laughter’ convert.","q":"It’s also clear in the passage that he maintains the identity of the noble and good ‘according to this argument, at least’, meaning ‘they are the same with reference to the subject, in that the subject escapes neither’."},{"s":582,"ri":339,"ph":"on the basis of this argument anyway","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus reports Damascius’ reading: noble, just and advantageous convert but keep distinct characters, as the virtues do, and are called ‘the same’ only insofar as they convert.","q":"Damascius interprets [the passage] differently, taking the view that just because the noble, just, and advantageous are the same with respect to their subject, it does not follow that they are the same with respect to their distinguishing character ( idiotês ),"},{"s":583,"ri":349,"ph":"Then just actions are beneficial, Alcibiades.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates names Alcibiades at the conclusion to rouse his attention and show that he, not Socrates, is the one drawing it.","q":"Since he introduced the conclusion [here], it is appropriate that he also mentioned Alcibiades’ name, in order to arouse his attention and show him that he is the one offering these [conclusions], and not Socrates."},{"s":584,"ri":353,"ph":"the just and the beneficial are the same","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":6,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' argument that this identification shows the dialogue concerns the soul, for only to the soul is the just advantageous, as Menoeceus' just but bodily ruinous death shows.","q":"for to [the soul] alone is the just advantageous, since it is not advantageous to the composite [of soul and body]: for example, the death of Menoeceus was just, since he died on behalf of his fatherland, but it was not advantageous to the body that perished."},{"s":584,"ri":353,"ph":"what could you do but laugh at him","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":531,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus notes that instead of abusing Alcibiades, Socrates puts the one who deserves ridicule in the position of laughing at others.","q":"He has put the person who is being ridiculed in the position of laughing at others."},{"s":584,"ri":353,"ph":"the Athenians or Peparethians","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":532,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says Peparethus, an insignificant island, is named to show that Attica’s famous name will not make the doubly ignorant knowledgeable about justice.","q":"He uses the phrase ‘ even the Peparethians ’ because Peparethus was an insignificant island;"},{"s":584,"ri":354,"ph":"I think one thing at one moment and a different thing the next","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":522,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades is now released from double ignorance but not yet at simple ignorance, standing in the borderland between them, sometimes thinking he knows and sometimes not.","q":"but he has not yet reached simple ignorance: rather, he is in the borderlands ( methorios ) between double and simple ignorance"},{"s":584,"ri":354,"ph":"But by the gods, Socrates, I do not even know what I am saying myself.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":532,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus finds the timing of the oath significant: Alcibiades swears only now, showing he is reduced to speechlessness.","q":"And the timing of his oath is not without significance; in fact, it shows that he is [now] in a speechless state."},{"s":584,"ri":357,"ph":"if you have two eyes or three, two hands or four","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":537,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates asks questions with an impossible answer, three eyes or four hands, not two eyes or one, where different answers might each be possible.","q":"For he didn’t ask ‘whether you have two eyes or one’ – since [either of these answers] would have been possible,"},{"s":585,"ri":358,"ph":"Well, I am anxious about myself at the moment.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":537,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades’ fear of answering even an obvious question: he has now felt the power of Socrates’ dialectic.","q":"Alcibiades is afraid of answering even such a clear question. And this is [natural] for someone who has been shown the abundance of Socrates’ dialectical power:"},{"s":586,"ri":367,"ph":"Do you know any means whereby you could go up into the sky?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":535,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus finds the example apt for Alcibiades, ward of ‘Olympian’ Pericles, and for the statesman, who must be raised above those in his care.","q":"the first [of ascending to the heavens], because it shows that it’s necessary for the statesman to be raised above those entrusted to his care;"},{"s":587,"ri":375,"ph":"you do not know about food preparation","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":535,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says cookery signifies that the statesman must be gentle towards those he advises, for they will not be persuaded unless they believe he guides them for their benefit.","q":"and the third case of cooking, because it’s necessary for the statesman to be gentle ( prosênês ) to those he advises in making his arguments."},{"s":588,"ri":379,"ph":"would you stay quiet and hand over to the helmsman?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":535,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus reads the helmsman example politically: as the helmsman takes thought for his ship, the statesman must exercise foresight for those in his charge.","q":"and the next case of the navigator because, just as the navigator exercises foresight on behalf of his ship, [the statesman] must exercise foresight on behalf of those entrusted to his care, even if he is above them;"},{"s":588,"ri":383,"ph":"errors in action are also due to this ignorance","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":537,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus thinks ‘this kind of ignorance’ well chosen: here only cognitive errors are meant, from double ignorance; errors also arise from another source, force.","q":"And presently we are examining [only] cognitive errors ( kata gnôsin ), where we speak of double and simple ignorance."},{"s":588,"ri":383,"ph":"this ignorance whereby you think you know when you do not know","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":6,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, counts this 'twofold ignorance', thinking one knows what one does not, among the topics that precede and prepare for the dialogue's principal design, self-knowledge.","q":"For the hypothesis of twofold ignorance, exhortation, and the like precede; but the demonstration of virtue and felicity, and the rejection of the multitude of arts, as being ignorant of themselves, of things pertaining to themselves, and in short of all things,—and every thing else of this kind, have a consequent order."},{"s":590,"ri":393,"ph":"who else is left except those who do not know but think that they know?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":523,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus spells out the argument: one who knows the road does not stray, one who knows he does not know does not set out; only the doubly ignorant wanders.","q":"this follows from the fact that [1] the person who knows the path doesn’t go astray, nor does [2] the person who, while not knowing [the path], recognises his ignorance (for this person would not even undertake the journey);"},{"s":590,"ri":395,"ph":"is this stupidity a matter of reproach?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":525,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says reproach falls on those who have but misuse: the simply ignorant fail to use innate reasons, the doubly ignorant use them badly and so are most reproachable.","q":"Likewise, the simple lack of knowledge ( amathia ) ‘deserves reproach’, since the person who lacks knowledge, despite possessing innate principles of reason ( logoi ) as part of his nature, fails to put those arguments to use;"},{"s":590,"ri":395,"ph":"is this stupidity a matter of reproach?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":537,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus sets ‘reproachable’ against ‘blessed’: blessedness belongs to the gods, free of death, so by being reproachable Alcibiades falls short of the divine.","q":"For reproachability is contrary to blessedness, and blessedness is appropriate to the gods, for whom the doom of death does not exist."},{"s":590,"ri":397,"ph":"is it then productive of the greatest evil and is it most dishonourable","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":526,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus explains that double ignorance ‘does most harm’ because it strives to draw false conclusions and to persuade others of what it does not know.","q":"And it ‘produces the most harm’ ( kakourgotatos ), since it strives to draw false conclusions and to persuade us of what it doesn’t know."},{"s":590,"ri":399,"ph":"can you name anything more important than what is just, noble, good and beneficial?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":537,"ref":"Lecture 14","n":"Olympiodorus says these are ‘greatest’ because everyone uses them from birth by a common concept, praising, blaming, accusing, defending, unlike specialised skills possessed by some only.","q":"He says that the just and good and advantageous are of ‘the greatest importance ( megista )’, since all people strive to make use of these from birth, working from a common concept;"},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"My, oh my, Alcibiades, what a predicament you are in!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":541,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates mourns Alcibiades as though dead, in Pythagorean fashion: they expelled unfit students, built them a cenotaph, and spoke of them as departed.","q":"This is Pythagorean [behaviour]: for the Pythagoreans used to cast out unsuitable [students] from their company ( homakoeion ) with their belongings, make a funeral monument ( kenotapheion ) for them, and mourn them and speak of them as if about the departed."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"My, oh my, Alcibiades, what a predicament you are in!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":541,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus explains the lament as purifying the vital part of the soul by forceful rebuke, after cross-examination has purified the cognitive part.","q":"the cognitive part is purified by cross-examination, but the vital part is purified by forceful correction ( epiplêxis )."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"I am reluctant to name it","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says the hesitation fits: the condition is unspeakable because it concerns matter, which is formless and below language, whereas the first cause is beyond language as beyond form.","q":"And it’s appropriate, too, that he said ‘I hesitate to call it by name’, since it is unspeakable ( aphrastos ) and cannot be captured by language ( arrhêtos ), inasmuch as this has to do with matter ( hulê ),"},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"since we are alone, it has to be said","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates criticises in private because a lover of reputation takes public criticism as hostility; hence philosophers’ custom of private correction.","q":"Because public criticism is interpreted as hostility by a person who is in the habit of caring about their reputation."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"you are wedded to the most extreme ignorance","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus praises ‘wedded’: as civil strife is worse than foreign war because nearer, so strife within one household is worse still; and the ignorance is ‘extreme’.","q":"then war between members of the same household is worse than civil strife to the same degree as they are nearer together."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"The argument convicts you and you convict yourself.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus explains the two witnesses: the argument, since in question and answer the respondent speaks, and Alcibiades himself, an invincible witness.","q":"[He means,] you have strong witnesses to this, since the argument convicts you (because it’s been shown that in a context of question and answer, the respondent is the speaker, not the questioner);"},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"you rush into politics before you have been educated","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus reads this as beliefs engendering a way of life: thinking he knows justice before learning it, Alcibiades hurries into public life.","q":"That is, ‘Your beliefs are engendering a way of life in you: since you’re doubly ignorant in supposing you know about justice before you’ve learned about it, you are hurrying into public life’."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"And you are not the only one in this plight","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says sharing the plight with many consoles Alcibiades but not Socrates, who as protector of humankind wanted everyone to be good.","q":"Now to Alcibiades, this is a consolation, to share his bad condition with many [others]: but this was no consolation to Socrates, since he wanted all people to be good, since he was the protector of humankind."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"perhaps your guardian, Pericles","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus asks why Pericles is praised here but later denied statesmanship, and reports Proclus’ answer: he lacks knowledge but is accepted as a man of true opinion.","q":"Now the philosopher Proclus says, ‘He refutes [Pericles] since he is not a man of knowledge ( epistêmôn ), but accepts him as a man of true belief ( orthodoxastikos )’."},{"s":592,"ri":405,"ph":"perhaps your guardian, Pericles","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":544,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus reports Damascius’ rejection, since right opinion acts like knowledge, and his solution: Pericles is accepted as a general, aiming at victory, but rejected as a statesman who made no citizens good.","q":"Hence his own solution of this puzzle is that [Socrates] accepts Pericles as a general ( stratêgos ), since [a general’s] goal is victory, but rejects him as a statesman, since he did not make his own citizens noble and good ( kaloi kai agathoi )."},{"s":592,"ri":406,"ph":"Anaxagoras","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":547,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says Anaxagoras helped Pericles to govern, having first introduced Intellect into things as a ruler set above them; so Pericles ruled the democracy like its intellect.","q":"[Pericles] was aided by Anaxagoras in governing ( to arkhein ), for this man was the first to introduce Intellect ( nous ) into things ( pragmata ), a ruler raised above what comes after it,"},{"s":592,"ri":406,"ph":"Pythoclides","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":547,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says Pythocles is named not merely as a musician but because he harmonised the citizens with suitable melodies.","q":"Pythocles is mentioned here not only as a musician, but because he harmonised the citizens [of Athens] using suitable melodies."},{"s":592,"ri":406,"ph":"he still consorts with Damon for this very purpose","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":547,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus says Damon too helped Pericles with melodies that harmonise citizens, as some melodies foster self-control, and Pericles visited him even on the threshold of old age.","q":"likewise there are melodies that harmonise the citizens, and Damon helped Pericles with these, using which he harmonised the city."},{"s":592,"ri":407,"ph":"being incapable of making another person wise in the same things as himself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":547,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus lists three marks of the wise: his teachers, his agreement with himself and the wise, and those he makes wise; the dialogue applies each, this last to Pericles.","q":"For there are three ways of distinguishing ( kharaktêristika ) wise people, [1] by those who precede them, [2] by those who keep company with them, and [3] by those who follow them."},{"s":594,"ri":420,"ph":"I think I am to blame for not being interested.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":547,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus notes three reasons in this exchange for failing to become wise: stupidity (Pericles’ sons), insanity (Clinias) and laziness (Alcibiades himself), and finds all three in a single line of poetry.","q":"Now notice, here are three reasons why one might fail to become wise: stupidity, insanity, and laziness."},{"s":595,"ri":421,"ph":"Callias, the son of Calliades","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":553,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus specifies that this is not the Callias of comedy, son of Hipponicus, but the son of Calliades.","q":"By ‘Callias’, he doesn’t mean the man who is described in the comedy, the son of Hipponicus; this is the son of Calliades."},{"s":595,"ri":421,"ph":"both wise and famous","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":553,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus glosses the word as ‘noteworthy’, well spoken of, not ‘eloquent’ in the later sense, since Plato also calls them wise.","q":"By ‘well-spoken’ ( ellogimos ) he means ‘noteworthy’ [i.e., well-spoken of ], and not what we mean by the word now, ‘eloquent’ – that’s clear, because he called him ‘wise’."},{"s":595,"ri":421,"ph":"having paid him one hundred minae","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":553,"ref":"Lecture 15","n":"Olympiodorus asks why a philosopher took fees, answering that Zeno taught contempt for money or redistributed it to poorer students, only pretending to take payment; Plato first refused fees.","q":"Well, we reply that he did this in order to accustom his students to despising money; or in order to provide funds to those in need, by taking them from those with plenty;"},{"s":595,"ri":423,"ph":"So be it. In that case, what have you in mind for yourself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":571,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘all right’ (eien) is the word of one encouraging, and ‘what will you do?’ is asked because Alcibiades is now refuted in both cognitive and vital parts.","q":"Now, the phrase ‘All right’ ( eien ) belongs to an encouraging person."},{"s":596,"ri":424,"ph":"Let us take counsel together, Socrates!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":574,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus says asking to deliberate shows Alcibiades has passed from double to simple ignorance, since neither the doubly ignorant nor the expert deliberates; he cites the verse ‘there’s need of a plan for me and you’.","q":"Up to this point, he was escaping double ignorance, and [now] he has arrived at simple [ignorance]. Consider: neither the doubly ignorant person, nor the knowledgeable expert, deliberates."},{"s":596,"ri":426,"ph":"after learning and having practised","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":574,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus divides the pair: learning concerns reason, practice or training concerns the non-rational, which is tamed by habituation.","q":"‘Learning’ is said about reason ( logos ), but ‘training’ about the unreasoning animals, since they are tamed by cultivation and training."},{"s":596,"ri":426,"ph":"take the trouble to learn","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":574,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘taking trouble’ involves both reason and the non-rational, in short our whole essence.","q":"[The phrase] ‘To go to the trouble’ ( pragmata ekhein ) has to do with reason and the non-rational, and basically with our entire essence ( ousia )."},{"s":596,"ri":426,"ph":"I know very well that by nature I shall be far superior to them","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":561,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus traces this to laziness and love of reputation: Alcibiades wants to avoid the labour of self-cultivation without seeming incapable of governing.","q":"And [he says this] because of both these concerns, namely, to avoid going to trouble over self-cultivation, and, for that reason, appearing incapable of setting the affairs of the state in order."},{"s":597,"ri":427,"ph":"What a thing to say","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":575,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘what a thing to say’ renders what Homer expresses indirectly in ‘what word has escaped the barrier of your teeth?’","q":"But the phrase ‘What a thing to say’ describes what the poet puts indirectly:"},{"s":597,"ri":427,"ph":"how unworthy of your character","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":579,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus takes the word rendered ‘character’ (idea) to mean outward appearance, contrasting fine looks with a mind lacking strength and courage.","q":"And he adds the phrase ‘unworthy of your fine form ( idea )’, meaning ‘appearance’,"},{"s":597,"ri":429,"ph":"I am troubled for you and for my own love","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":574,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus explains the double grief: for Alcibiades because he proves so petty, and for himself because he loves someone so petty.","q":"about you, because you turn out to be so petty ( smikroprepês ); and about myself, because I love someone so petty’."},{"s":599,"ri":435,"ph":"if you were intending to steer a trireme about to go into battle","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":562,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades mistakes allies for rivals, like a pilot treating his sailors as rivals rather than joining them against winds and storms; the trireme suits the military theme.","q":"as if a ship’s pilot were to regard the sailors as his rivals instead of allies, rather than joining them in looking to other adversaries, to the chance movement of the winds and storms."},{"s":601,"ri":443,"ph":"Meidias, the quail-fighter","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":588,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus explains the nickname: this Meidias reared quails for fighting; he adds a dig at Alcibiades, from whose robes a quail once escaped while he addressed the people.","q":"was called ‘quail-flicker’ because he reared quails for fighting."},{"s":601,"ri":443,"ph":"Meidias, the quail-fighter","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":591,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus warns that this quail-rearing Meidias is not the Meidias who slapped Demosthenes, who was born later; Socrates means an earlier man.","q":"[Socrates] doesn’t mean by ‘Meidias’ the person who slapped Demosthenes;"},{"s":602,"ri":444,"ph":"the Spartan generals and the Persian King are no better than the rest","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":564,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus notes Alcibiades belittles his rivals by renaming them: Socrates says ‘kings of the Spartans’ and ‘Great King’, Alcibiades ‘generals’ and merely ‘the Persian king’.","q":"Hence, whereas Socrates, playing up the stature of [Alcibiades’] enemies, once calls them ‘Great King’, and elsewhere ‘Kings of the Lacedaemonians’, Alcibiades calls them ‘the Lacedaemonian generals’, and instead of the ‘Great King’, just ‘king’."},{"s":603,"ri":447,"ph":"through fearing them and regarding them as formidable","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":611,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus sees a counterfactual: even if the rivals are unworthy, we should regard them as worthy so as to cultivate ourselves, an argument like Aristotle’s Protrepticus on philosophising.","q":"Notice how he uses a counterfactual here, to the effect that even if they are actually unworthy of notice, all the same we should consider them [worthy], in order that we will cultivate ourselves."},{"s":603,"ri":447,"ph":"through fearing them and regarding them as formidable","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":564,"ref":"Lecture 16","n":"Olympiodorus finds Platonic doctrine here: the Good, not Intellect, is first principle, since the Good lends itself even to a useful untruth, treating the false as true for the sake of good.","q":"likewise here, [we have] the notion of taking something untrue as true for the sake of the good."},{"s":604,"ri":455,"ph":"Are better natures likely to arise among high-born families or not?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":627,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus affirms that those from well-born families are almost always better, citing Homer’s horses bred from Laomedon’s stock without his knowledge.","q":"For in virtually every case, those from wellborn families are better;"},{"s":604,"ri":459,"ph":"comparing our own lineage to theirs","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":629,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates compares the Persians not with Athens but with himself and Alcibiades together, as a lover who wishes to ascend with his beloved, not alone.","q":"[That’s because] as a lover ( erôtikos ), he acts in a manner that draws the young man in ( oikeiopoieitai ): for the lover does not want to ascend alone, but along with his beloved."},{"s":604,"ri":459,"ph":"the other from Achaemenes","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":616,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus gives the genealogy: Perseus was son of Zeus and Danaë, Achaemenes son of Perseus and Andromeda, whence the Persian kings are called Achaemenids.","q":"for Perseus was the son of Zeus and Danaë, and Achaemenes was the son of Perseus and Andromeda (the one in heaven), which is also why the Persian [kings] are called ‘Achaemenids’."},{"s":604,"ri":459,"ph":"trace their lineage back to Perseus, son of Zeus","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus’ puzzle why the Spartans are traced to Perseus rather than Heracles, and his answer: Perseus, also a purifier of evils, was honoured first because winged.","q":"And he resolves [the puzzle] himself, saying that [Plato] probably honoured [Perseus] before [Heracles] because he was winged:"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"be persuaded by me and by the inscription at Delphi, 'Know thyself'","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":4,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Proclus cites this exhortation among his arguments that the target of the whole dialogue is self-knowledge.","q":"Next [he establishes that the target is something like this] on the basis of certain expressions in the dialogue; for example, it is stated in it 'But, my good friend, being persuaded by me and by the inscription at Delphi, \"Know Thyself\". '"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"no one, unless he happens to be a lover of yours, cares about your birth","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus notes Socrates should have said no one cares for Alcibiades’ cultivation, but, philosophers liking generality, says no one cares for him except a lover.","q":"but because philosophers are fond of generalisation, he says that ‘No one is paying attention to you , unless it’s someone happens to be in love with you’."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"if you wish to cast an eye on the wealth and luxury","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":635,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus hears disgust in the hesitant ‘if you wish’: Socrates dislikes comparing Alcibiades by lifestyle at all, since if the human being is the soul such bodily criteria are improper.","q":"Now [Socrates] is generally disgusted ( duskherainei ) with the criteria of comparison that he uses here for Alcibiades (for if the human being is the soul, we should not make comparisons on these criteria)"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"we should not let that go without a mention either","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":635,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus contrasts this phrase with the milder ‘if you wish’: wealth lies even further outside us than bodily lifestyle, so Socrates' reluctance and disgust grow stronger when he turns to it.","q":"For lifestyle ( diaita ) concerns the body, but wealth concerns what derives from outside [the body]; hence in the case of lifestyle he uses the words ‘if you wish’ [122C4] but in the case of wealth, ‘we can’t leave this unspoken’ [122D2]."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the wealth and luxury, the clothes and flowing garments","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":647,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates speaks here like an orator in a mocking tone, putting everything in the plural, ‘wealths’ and ‘luxuries’, instead of the singular.","q":"Note, too, how Socrates chooses the right moment to speak like an orator and use a mocking tone, setting everything out in plurals instead of singular forms, using the words ‘wealths’ and ‘luxuries’."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"flowing garments","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":647,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus explains the ‘trailing robes’ as the Persian tunics reaching to the feet, which is why Persians are called robe-trailers; Indians and Ionians dress likewise.","q":"And he speaks about ‘trailing robes’ (using two words) because the Persians wear tunics that stretch to their feet, which is also why they’re called ‘robe-trailers’ ( helkesipeploi ); as do the Indians, like the Ionians too."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the vast retinue of servants","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":647,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus observes that Socrates uses plural forms for the train of attendants, though a train is singular, in order to magnify the Persians.","q":"Notice as well how he introduces plural forms to elevate [the Persians], even though the train [of servants] is singular."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the self-restraint and orderliness","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus asks how Spartan women can be called self-controlled, given the comic jibes against them, and answers that they obey their masters, self-control being the inferior obeying the superior.","q":"Well, he says that the Laconian women are self-controlled because they are obedient to their masters."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"their love for work","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus glosses love of work by the Spartan custom of whipping boys from the age of four, to habituate them to hardship.","q":"For the Laconians were also fond of hardship ( philoponoi ), which is why from childhood they were whipped, habituating them for hard work; for they received whippings from four years of age."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"victory and honour","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates ends the list with love of honour because the Spartans lived under a timocracy, a constitution governed by honour.","q":"And notice how he finished with love of honour ( philotimia ), since they lived under that kind of constitution, namely, under a timocracy."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"their ownership of slaves in general and of helots, horses too","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":637,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus sorts Spartan wealth into three kinds: unmoved (the Messenian land), self-moving (helots and horses) and moved by another (gold and tribute), and finds Alcibiades outdone in each.","q":"Consider that wealth is threefold: [1] self-moving ( autokinêtos ), [2] moved by another ( heterokinêtos ), or [3] not moving at all ( akinêtos ): in every one of these cases the young man is worsted by the wealth of the Lacedaemonians."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"their own and that of the Messenians","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus says the sentence must be construed in reverse order: Sparta has so much land, of its own and in Messene, that no Athenian estate could rival its size or excellence.","q":"Also, the language should be interpreted in reverse: ‘For they have so much land, both in size and excellence, of their own and in Messene, that not one [of our estates] could compete with’."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"gold and silver have flowed into Sparta from all of the Greeks","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus takes these words as a reproach: only taking and never giving is a sign of envy and marks wealth as material, since matter is purely receptive.","q":"Note how it’s also possible to show from these words that Plato really reproaches [the Lacedaemonians], since it’s a sign of envy only to take and never to give. In fact, he represents their wealth as enmeshed in matter ( enhulos );"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the story of Aesop where the fox speaks to the lion","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":641,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus retells the fable of the sick old lion and the fox who saw tracks going in but none coming out, and says Sparta is likened to the aged lion because its aristocracy had weakened.","q":"And he compares them to the lion who has grown old, since they live according to a meritocracy ( aristokrateia ), which is appropriate to the lion, but they weakened it, so that they were at risk of declining from a meritocracy into oligarchy."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the footprints of the money going in to Sparta","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Plato puts ‘money’ in place of the lion of the fable, likening money to a wild beast.","q":"He changed the name of the lion into ‘money’; for he likened [money] to a wild beast."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"I once heard a trustworthy man","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus reports that the commentators identify the trustworthy traveller as Xenophon, who described Persia after marching with Cyrus against Artaxerxes.","q":"[The commentators] explain that he is talking about Xenophon: for this man related his inquiry into Persian [life] in the Anabasis , after joining Cyrus in his expedition against his brother Artaxerxes."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the girdle of the king’s wife","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":652,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus defends the story of cities assigned to the queen's ornaments by comparison with Tyre, which gives its name to purple because it is set aside for the emperor's purple.","q":"There’s nothing implausible ( atopos ) about cities being allocated for each and every ornament worn by the queens of Persia, since among us, too, purple is called ‘Tyrian’, [Tyre] being the source of purple dye, because it’s allocated especially for the Emperor’s purple toga."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"have been selected for the adornment of the woman","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":653,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus holds that by this talk of ornaments Socrates does not praise the Persians but dismisses them as women, who love wealth through low birth and natural weakness.","q":"Notice, too, how he used this kind of talk to dismiss them as women: for [women] love wealth because of their low birth and natural weakness."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"The son of Dinomache","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":653,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus finds the phrase emphatic: after building up the Persian royal line through three generations, Socrates sets against it simply ‘the son of Dinomache’.","q":"This is emphatic; for where he had previously elevated his speech by basing it on prior, contemporary, and posterior generations, now he says, ‘The son of Dinomache’."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"when he is not yet fully twenty years of age","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":655,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus finds this emphatic and, citing a verse that ranks the man who sees for himself first and the one who heeds good advice second, puts Alcibiades in the third rank.","q":"But Alcibiades has been cast down into the third rank, since he does not even pay heed to someone else."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"after he has learned, cared for himself and practised","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":659,"ref":"Lecture 18","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes the terms: learning concerns reason, training the non-rational soul, while care or cultivation is common to both.","q":"‘learning’ referred to reason, but ‘training’ ( askêsis ) to the non-rational soul."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"So, blessed man","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":688,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates calls Alcibiades ‘blessed’ because he is about to link him to the gods, deriving makarios from a negating ma- and kêr, fate.","q":"And he calls [Alcibiades] ‘blessed’ ( makarion ) because he is about to link him to the gods; and in this context, [the word] ‘blessedness’ ( makariotês ) is used for the negation of ‘fatedness’ ( kêr ), since [the prefix] ma– indicates negation."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"be persuaded by me and by the inscription at Delphi","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":688,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus explains that Socrates names himself before the god because he is the proximate cause of Alcibiades' salvation.","q":"And he positions himself before the god because he is the proximate cause of Alcibiades’ salvation."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"be persuaded by me and by the inscription at Delphi","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":696,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates here allies himself with the god, as he calls himself a fellow-slave of the swans and here sides with Apollo against Spartan love of money.","q":"Thus in the Phaedrus [ sic ; Phaedo 85B] he calls himself ‘a fellow-slave of the swans’; so too in the present dialogue he allied himself with Apollo, criticising the Lacedaemonians for their love of money"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"the inscription at Delphi, ‘Know thyself’","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":688,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus reads ‘know’ as showing that we are neither body nor compound, since neither knows, but soul, and not the vegetative soul.","q":"[1] he used the word ‘know’ ( gnôthi ) [to show] that we are not a body ( sôma ) (for [a body] does not have knowledge), nor a combination [of body and soul]"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"‘Know thyself’","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":688,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus reads ‘thyself’ as showing that we are rational soul, which reverts upon itself, yet not always fully rational, which is why the knowing must be commanded.","q":"and [2] he used the word ‘thyself’ ( seauton ) since [we are] also not non-rational ( alogos ) (for the non-rational [soul] does not revert upon itself), but [rather] rational, and not always completely rational, but sometimes even ignorant."},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"know that our adversaries are these kings and not those whom you regard as such","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":698,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus paraphrases: the Persians and Spartans are the real competitors, not the Athenians Alcibiades had in mind, who turned out to be allies.","q":"‘For the Persians and Laconians are our competitors and adversaries, “but not the ones you thought”’ (for those [Athenians] turned out to be allies);"},{"s":606,"ri":-1,"ph":"you will be found wanting in reputation among the Greeks and non-Greeks","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":698,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus sees Socratic therapy of like by like: appealing to Alcibiades' love of fame, Socrates tells him that only civic knowledge can make him famous.","q":"[Here is] Socratic therapy, to say ‘If you aim to become famous, learn what civic knowledge ( politikê epistêmê ) is; for that alone has the capacity to make you famous’."},{"s":606,"ri":461,"ph":"mine goes back to Daedalus, and Daedalus to Hephaestus, son of Zeus","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":616,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains the jest: Daedalus first parted statues’ feet, representing self-movement, and Socrates first made men self-moving, bringing them to knowledge by midwifery and discovery rather than instruction.","q":"and Socrates was the first to make humans self-moving, since he did not allow them to come to knowledge by instruction, but by midwifery and discovery, and in this way he made them self-moving."},{"s":606,"ri":461,"ph":"the ancestry of Eurysaces and his fatherland, Salamis","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":617,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates shows Alcibiades is not even originally Athenian: tracing his line through Ajax leads to Aegina and Telamon of Salamis, father of Eurysaces.","q":"he says that ‘You are not originally Athenian, Alcibiades, if you trace your lineage from Ajax; for Ajax was from Aegina, and Ajax was [descended] from Telamon of Salamis, and it’s from this man that Eurysaces was born’;"},{"s":606,"ri":461,"ph":"kings of Argos and Sparta","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus says these words describe the Peloponnese, anciently divided into five lands: the Argolid, Messene, Arcadia, Elis and Laconia.","q":"He uses these words to describe the Peloponnese, because this [land] was long ago divided into five parts:"},{"s":607,"ri":461,"ph":"kings whose wives are guarded in public by the Ephors","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":619,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains the guarding by an oracle warning Sparta against a king ‘lame in lineage’, not of the Heraclids, fulfilled in Leotychidas, born of Alcibiades’ affair with Agis’ wife.","q":"The Lacedaemonians guarded them because they received an oracle that they would have ill fortune if they put forward a king ‘lame in lineage’, that is, one who did not belong to the line of the sons of Heracles."},{"s":608,"ri":461,"ph":"the king’s wife is protected by nothing apart from fear","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":619,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the Persians set no guard on the queen, judging dread of the consequent punishments a sufficient guard.","q":"But among the Persians, they did not consider it worthwhile to set a guard [on the Queen], since they felt that the terror of consequent punishments was a sufficient guard."},{"s":608,"ri":461,"ph":"all Asia sacrifices and celebrates the birthday of the king","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains the annual birthday feast by Persian reverence for the heavens, above all the Sun, which is the symbol of the year.","q":"That’s why all of them immediately conduct a sacrifice, then honour the king’s birthday each and every year, because the Persians honour what is heavenly, and most of all in the heavens the Sun;"},{"s":608,"ri":461,"ph":"as the comic poet says, not even the neighbours were really aware of it","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the source of the line about the neighbours not noticing the birth: it is a quotation from Plato the comic poet, not the philosopher.","q":"This is a citation 121D from Plato the comic poet."},{"s":609,"ri":461,"ph":"by shaping and straightening the child’s limbs","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":621,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus says the eunuchs moulded the prince’s body for beauty, even making his nose aquiline like the kingly eagle, a fashion begun from Cyrus.","q":"and they mould his body parts with an eye to beauty, even making his nose aquiline, showing that the boy is fit for leadership and kingship’."},{"s":609,"ri":461,"ph":"Once the children reach seven years of age","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains why training changes at seven: seven is a critical number, a seven-month embryo is viable, and hepta sounds like septa, ‘revered’.","q":"For at seven years of age, their cultivation changed: this is because seven is respected as a critical moment ( krisimos ), and because a seven-month-old [embryo] is considered viable ( zôsimos ),"},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"the wisest, the most just, the most sound-minded and the bravest","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":623,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus says the Persians taught the habituated virtues, not natural ones (unteachable) nor civic ones, which convert so that one teacher would suffice; habituated virtues are taught separately.","q":"rather, it was the habituated ( êthikai ) [forms of excellence], for these are taught and are not mutually convertible."},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"the wisest, the most just, the most sound-minded and the bravest","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains the order: wisdom first, belonging to reason, our most valuable part, and justice next, since it pervades our whole being.","q":"He ranks wisdom ( sophia ) first, since it belongs to the more valuable part of us, our reason ( logos ); justice ( dikaiosunê ) comes immediately afterward, since it interpenetrates our entire being ( ousia )."},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"and this is the worship of gods","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus says Plato adds ‘worship of the gods’ so no one takes the Magi’s art for spells and sorcery; the gods meant are the heavenly bodies the Persians honoured.","q":"to prevent anyone imagining that by ‘the Magi’s art’ ( mageia ) he means spells ( manganeia ) and sorcery ( goêteia ), he adds ‘which is the cultivation ( therapeia ) of the gods’."},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"he also teaches the business of a king","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus infers that if kingship is teachable it should go by expertise, not by lot, and kingship by lot should not be accepted.","q":"If kingship is teachable, then it is [properly] not assigned by lot, but according to expertise ( epistêmê ), and we should not accept kingship that’s assigned by lot."},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"The most just teaches him to speak the truth throughout his entire life.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus explains why justice teaches truthfulness: justice concerns words as well as deeds, and the just man, giving each its due in speech, speaks truth, not lies.","q":"And the just person portions out what each deserves: therefore, when he portions out what is deserved in speech, he will speak the truth; for he does not portion out lies."},{"s":610,"ri":461,"ph":"never to be ruled by a single pleasure","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus comments that it is strange for one who has defeated the enemy to be conquered by the spoils, such as captive women.","q":"For it is a strange business if a person who has defeated the enemy is then conquered by their spoils, if that happens to be women in their prime."},{"s":611,"ri":461,"ph":"telling him that he is a slave whenever he is afraid","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus links slavery and fear etymologically, deriving doulos, ‘slave’, from deos, ‘fear’: we are free only when fearing nothing.","q":"It is when we are not under [another’s] control that we are truly free, fearing nothing, since the word ‘slave’ ( doulos ) is derived from ‘fear’ ( deos );"},{"s":611,"ri":461,"ph":"utterly useless on account of his old age","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus adds that Zopyrus was useless not only through age but also because maimed, the only sort Pericles would assign; tutors in his own day are similar.","q":"[He was] not only perfectly useless because of his old age, but also because he was maimed"},{"s":611,"ri":461,"ph":"I would also have described the general development and education of your adversaries","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":630,"ref":"Lecture 17","n":"Olympiodorus identifies the figure of omission (paraleipsis), which magnifies both speech and speaker and hints silently at something greater.","q":"[This is the rhetorical] figure of omission ( paraleipsis ), which enlarges both the speech and the speaker, and hints silently at something greater."},{"s":621,"ri":462,"ph":"Can you elaborate?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":698,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades, in asking Socrates to explain, treats him as an ‘interpreter’, the title of Apollo whose oracles saved the Greeks, since Socrates had allied himself with the god.","q":"for he calls [Socrates] an ‘interpreter’ ( exêgêtês ), because Apollo was honoured by the Athenians as an interpreter, since through the interpretation of his oracles he became a cause of salvation for the Greeks."},{"s":622,"ri":463,"ph":"we really should deliberate together","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":698,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates feigns ignorance and deliberates jointly although he knows, because a lover chooses to share his beloved's condition.","q":"But it’s because he is a lover that he acts ignorant in company with his beloved."},{"s":622,"ri":463,"ph":"I am not just referring to you and excluding myself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":690,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus explains Socrates' self-ignorance by the many grades of self-knowledge: Socrates did not yet know himself in the highest, inspired way.","q":"Now Socrates did not know himself as an inspired person, which is also why in the Phaedrus he says, ‘it is ridiculous to look into other things, while remaining ignorant of myself’ [229E–230A]."},{"s":622,"ri":465,"ph":"My guardian is better and wiser than your Pericles.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":699,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus asks whether Alcibiades lacked a divine guardian, since all have an allotted daimon, and answers that he lacked conscious awareness of it, as Socrates had.","q":"But [Alcibiades does not have such a daimon] with awareness of it ( sun aisthêseôs ), as Socrates does."},{"s":622,"ri":467,"ph":"A god, Alcibiades","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":108,"ref":"Lecture 3","n":"Olympiodorus cites this answer as proof that Socrates' allotted daimon was divine, one of three points the commentators single out about Socrates' daimon.","q":"Second, that his allotted daimon was divine, and he makes this clear: for he says in the present dialogue that 'My guardian is better and wiser than yours' [124C], and when the young man asks, 'Who is that?' he replies, 'A god, Alcibiades, a god.'"},{"s":622,"ri":467,"ph":"you will never become illustrious except through me","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":699,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus notes that the word for becoming illustrious, ‘manifestation’, properly belongs to a god, and is used of Alcibiades because the lover of reputation loves to be manifest.","q":"He used the same word, ‘manifestation’ ( epiphaneia ), both for the god and for Alcibiades; because in its proper sense ‘manifestation’ is said of a god."},{"s":623,"ri":468,"ph":"You are joking, Socrates!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":699,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades thinks Socrates is joking because he claimed to need cultivation too; Socrates' ‘perhaps’ concedes play, as activity about secondary matters is a game.","q":"[Alcibaides] says that he is ‘being playful’ because he said ‘I’m in need of cultivation too’."},{"s":623,"ri":469,"ph":"all men do but we two need it very much","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":703,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus contrasts Achilles' wish that all others perish and he and Patroclus survive with Socrates, guardian of human nature, who says all humans need cultivation.","q":"For he says, ‘let everyone else perish, but let us be saved’, whereas Socrates, as the guardian of human nature, says that all human beings stand in need of a great deal of cultivation."},{"s":623,"ri":469,"ph":"all men do but we two need it very much","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":694,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus explains why Alcibiades especially needs care: great natures do great harm uncultivated and great good cultivated, like rich land that yields thistles if left unfarmed.","q":"first, Alcibiades [does], because great natures become causes of great harm ( kaka ) when they go without cultivation, just as when they happen to be cultivated, they are the causes of great goods."},{"s":623,"ri":473,"ph":"We should neither give up nor prove cowards","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":703,"ref":"Lecture 19","n":"Olympiodorus refers ‘no giving up’ to the argument, which must not be enfeebled, and ‘no slacking’ to appetite, which must be kept taut towards benefit.","q":"‘There must be no giving up’, with a view to the argument ( logos ), for we must not have the argument enfeebled with its sinews cut; ‘nor slacking off’, with a view to appetite ( epithumia ), for we must not let our appetite slacken, but keep it taut, straining for the aim of benefit ( ôphelia )."},{"s":624,"ri":475,"ph":"we wish to become as excellent as possible","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus takes this question as the prelude to the inquiry, where Socrates begins to describe the material with which the statesman's knowledge deals.","q":"And at this point [Socrates] began the prelude to his work ( prooimion tês praxeôs ), by describing the material of the statesman’s knowledge."},{"s":624,"ri":478,"ph":"The very excellence by which men are good men, of course.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus faults the young Alcibiades' answer for not specifying which kind of excellence he meant, whether concerned with objects of contemplation or with actions.","q":"For he ought to have described the [specific] kind of excellence required, whether it is concerned with objects of contemplation ( theôrêmata ), or with actions ( prakta )."},{"s":624,"ri":479,"ph":"Men who are good in what?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the text proceeds by question and answer, not orderly exposition, because Socrates as midwife wants Alcibiades to bring out the answer with him.","q":"For the text ( lexis ) doesn’t proceed exactly like the conceptual survey ( theôria ), providing [the account of] the material of the statesman in good order, but [it proceeds] by question and answer, because Socrates doesn’t want to describe the material himself, but in concert with Alcibiades, as a midwife ( hôs maieutikos );"},{"s":624,"ri":480,"ph":"Obviously in performing actions.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades has climbed one rung, grasping that practical matters are the statesman's material, though as an undivided whole, so Socrates asks which kind.","q":"for he took the argument as a single, undivided whole maintaining that practical [matters] serve as material for statesmen."},{"s":624,"ri":489,"ph":"Those performed by the noble and good among the Athenians.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus allows Alcibiades only one gain here: that he treats ‘noble’ and ‘good’ as convertible terms.","q":"Alcibiades was benefited in this respect alone, that he converts the noble and good ( antistrephei kalon kai agathon )."},{"s":626,"ri":504,"ph":"So by this argument anyway, the same person is bad and also good.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus says being good at one craft and bad at another is unremarkable in a shoemaker but absurd in the statesman, who imitates the Demiurge and must benefit every practical concern.","q":"And there’s nothing out of place about this in the case of the shoemaker, but it is out of place in the case of the statesman who wishes to produce a benefit for every practical concern ( prakta ), because he is putting on the persona ( hupoduetai ) of the Demiurge."},{"s":626,"ri":509,"ph":"I mean those who are able to rule in the city.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":742,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus marks Alcibiades' answer as the point where Socrates turns from the statesman's material to his form, which is ruling.","q":"At this point, he begins to explain the form ( eidos ) of the statesman, namely, rulership ( to arkhein )."},{"s":627,"ri":522,"ph":"he rules over people who are dealing with people","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":737,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus says statesmanship is rule not over animals, nor over the sick, reapers, sailors or singers, but over human beings as they deal with human beings.","q":"and it’s not of human beings simply, since the doctor also rules human beings in distress; nor of human beings when they reap (for that’s the work of farming); nor of sailors (for that’s the pilot’s work); nor of singers (for that’s the chorus-teacher’s work); but of human beings ‘as they deal with ( khrômenôn ) human beings’ [125C]."},{"s":627,"ri":524,"ph":"Is it over boatswains who are dealing with rowers?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":737,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus explains that pilots and chorus-masters also rule people who rule others, so Socrates specifies that the statesman rules people engaged in civic life and contractual agreements.","q":"But since the pilot also rules people who are boatswains (it’s people who look after the ship’s tackle who are called ‘boatswains’), who rule the people who row, and the chorus-teacher rules the flute-players who lead the singers, therefore [Socrates] adds that the statesman rules human beings, those who are in positions of leadership, and who join together in contractual agreements."},{"s":629,"ri":539,"ph":"I would call it sound judgement, Socrates.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":759,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus faults the answer: Alcibiades named good counsel, an activity, where he should have named the state, practical wisdom, and did not say which kind of good counsel.","q":"named practical wisdom, but he named good advice [instead];"},{"s":631,"ri":548,"ph":"through the presence of sight and the absence of blindness","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":771,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus asks how human skill can remove blindness, which belongs to divine radiance, and answers that ‘blindness’ here names the condition caused by cataracts.","q":"[The explanation is] that it’s possible to use the name ‘blindness’ for the condition that is caused by cataracts."},{"s":632,"ri":551,"ph":"this happens when they have friendship towards one another","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":771,"ref":"Lecture 20","n":"Olympiodorus sees Alcibiades, after many examples, giving birth to the statesman's final cause, friendship, but only in basic form, which Socrates will make him refine.","q":"Socrates has used many examples to explain to the young man what the statesman’s final cause [or purpose] is, and at this point, Alcibiades gives birth to this [answer], saying that it is ‘to produce friendship among his citizens’."},{"s":632,"ri":552,"ph":"by friendship do you mean agreement or disagreement?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":775,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates challenges the answer not because friendship is not the goal, but because he suspects Alcibiades fails to distinguish affection from friendship.","q":"Socrates challenges him – not because he himself disagrees that friendship is the goal, but because he suspected that Alcibiades did not distinguish between affection ( storgê ) and friendship ( philia )."},{"s":632,"ri":552,"ph":"by friendship do you mean agreement","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":777,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades should have seen that friendship has many senses: union with the One, agreement of mind, like-thinking, shared opinion, shared feeling and kinship.","q":"But Alcibiades ought to have considered that [the word] ‘friendship’ ( philia ) is said in many ways."},{"s":632,"ri":554,"ph":"through what skill do the cities agree about number?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":779,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus notes that with arithmetic Socrates moves from many to one, cities, then individuals, then each person with himself, since multitude suits number.","q":"And in the case of arithmetic, he began from a multitude and arrived at one, when he said ‘cities agree with one another about numbers thanks to arithmetic’, then ‘private citizens’ do, and ‘each person with himself’ [126C]."},{"s":632,"ri":554,"ph":"through what skill do the cities agree about number?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":822,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says the craft examples prove a friendship of understanding without affection: craftsmen agree about their objects of knowledge, yet as men envy one another.","q":"Notice how he proves the second point in this [passage] – that there is a [kind of] friendship according to understanding ( gnôsis ), not according to affection – using [the example] of skilled craftspeople:"},{"s":632,"ri":560,"ph":"which is greater, a span or a cubit? Is it not through measurement?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":779,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus notes that with measurement Socrates reverses the order, from one person to many, because the one suits geometry, which deals with continuous quantity.","q":"But in the case of geometrical measurement, he did the reverse, beginning from the one and arriving at the many, when he said, ‘What about size? [Isn’t it geometry] that makes a person agree with himself, and private citizens with one another, and cities’?"},{"s":633,"ri":564,"ph":"does not the same also apply to weighing?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":779,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus finds here a third species of quantity, weight, distinct from continuous quantity, which is why Socrates uses neither ordering of one and many for it.","q":"In the case of weighing, on the other hand, he didn’t use any of this language, neither starting from the one and arriving at the many, nor [starting] from the many and arriving at the one, since this is a different case than those [of arithmetic and geometry]."},{"s":633,"ri":566,"ph":"what is the agreement you refer to, what is it about","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":783,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus asks why Socrates, who blamed Polus for it, asks many questions at once, and answers that they form one continuous question, and that interrogative inquiry permits extended answers.","q":"But you should offer another solution ( lusis ), as well: one should not ask multiple questions [at once] in [strict] dialectical questioning, where there’s no place for [extended] speech ( logos ), but it’s sufficient to nod or shake your head and say ‘yes’ or ‘no’;"},{"s":633,"ri":566,"ph":"in relation to itself and in relation to another","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":785,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus maps the clauses onto three figures: the city to the statesman, the individual with himself to the person of good character, and with another to the household manager.","q":"For by saying ‘Is it the same in a state’, he pointed to the statesman; but by saying ‘an individual with himself’, he pointed to the person of good character; and by saying ‘and with another’, to the household manager."},{"s":633,"ri":566,"ph":"what is the agreement you refer to, what is it about","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":822,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus defends asking several questions at once in interrogative inquiry by Socrates' own practice in the Apology, where Callias answers each in turn.","q":"At any rate, [Socrates] himself asks many questions [at once] in the Apology : ‘Who is he, and where is he from, and what’s the price [of his teaching]?’ [ Ap. 20B]."},{"s":633,"ri":567,"ph":"Yes, that is likely anyway.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":785,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades could grant that statesman, household manager and good person are one because the dialogue had shown that whoever persuades one persuades many.","q":"Well, the present dialogue helped him, thanks to the argument that ran, ‘The person who is persuasive to one is also persuasive to many’ [cf. 114B–D]."},{"s":633,"ri":567,"ph":"Yes, that is likely anyway.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":822,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades, granting that statesman, household manager and good person differ only in scale, outdoes Aristotle, who distinguishes them, though only with Socrates' help.","q":"In order, however, that we not cut down Aristotle’s worthy reputation ( axiôma ), let us say that Alcibiades is superior to Aristotle, not [when he acts] by himself, but when he acts in concert with Socrates."},{"s":633,"ri":568,"ph":"Do not be reluctant to answer.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":782,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates, aware he has asked many questions, fittingly urges Alcibiades not to flag in answering.","q":"For these reasons, and since he himself is aware that he asked a lot of questions, he says ‘Don’t flag in your answers’ ( mê kamês apokrinomenos ); and the words ‘don’t flag’ came out at the right moment, since there were so many questions."},{"s":634,"ri":569,"ph":"a father and mother agree in loving a son","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":822,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says these examples show Alcibiades identifying friendship with affection, and that, unable to grasp the form, he descended to examples.","q":"except, thanks to his error on this point, he could not grasp the form of affection, but descended to [the level] of examples;"},{"s":634,"ri":570,"ph":"about woolspinning","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":785,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus derives the word for wool-working either from working wool or from wool-baskets, and connects it with the ‘labouring’ mules.","q":"And the skill of carding wool ( xantikê ) is either called ‘wool-working’ ( talasiourgia ) from the working of wool ( ta lasia ergazesthai ), or from the baskets ( talaroi );"},{"s":634,"ri":570,"ph":"when he is not knowledgeable and she is knowledgeable","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":822,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus finds the contrast between the man who does not know and the woman who does well chosen, since anyone can learn to weave wool.","q":"And the words ‘the man who does not know with the woman who does know’ were well chosen: for anyone can learn to weave wool."},{"s":634,"ri":572,"ph":"for this is a feminine branch of learning","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":787,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus asks why a subject is called feminine if men and women share excellences, and answers that this holds only under the present kind of constitution.","q":"Now if men and women have common areas of excellence ( aretê ), why does he say ‘for this is a woman’s area of learning’? The answer is, [that’s true] under this [present] kind of constitution."},{"s":635,"ri":576,"ph":"For you would probably say that this, in turn, is a masculine pursuit.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":787,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus finds ‘perhaps’ and ‘you would say’ apt, since Socrates himself would reject the claim, holding that men's and women's natures and excellences are shared.","q":"The language ‘perhaps’ and ‘you would say’ is appropriate [i.e., because Socrates himself would reject these claims]."},{"s":635,"ri":578,"ph":"So according to your argument, there are manly and womanly subjects.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":824,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says the division of tasks by sex does not reflect the best constitution, and Socrates signals this by saying ‘according to your argument’ and ‘perhaps’.","q":"It’s because this remark doesn’t correspond to the best kind of constitution ( hê eu ekhousa politeia ), but to another kind; moreover, he says ‘according to your argument ( logos )’ [127A], and ‘perhaps’, and so on."},{"s":635,"ri":581,"ph":"Nor is there friendship either, if, in fact, friendship is agreement.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus casts the inference as a syllogism in the second figure concluding that husband and wife are not friends.","q":"And the syllogism is structured in the second figure as follows: ‘A woman and man do not agree (for they are not concerned with the same objects of knowledge); those who are friends to one another agree (since it has been proven that disagreement is enmity); therefore, a man and woman are not friends to another’."},{"s":636,"ri":586,"ph":"are cities not well organised either, whenever each person does what belongs to himself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus calls this a second paradox, that the just do not govern cities well, and analyses it into two pre-syllogisms and a final syllogism.","q":"here he also adds a second paradox to his argument, namely, that the just do not govern their cities well."},{"s":637,"ri":590,"ph":"This was not so a moment ago!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says that in reproaching Alcibiades' change of position Socrates analyses deductively what he had earlier shown synthetically.","q":"And in these words, he analyzes [deductively] what he showed synthetically earlier."},{"s":638,"ri":596,"ph":"what precisely do you mean by the friendship and agreement","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus reads the question as an invitation to Alcibiades to withdraw whichever premiss he likes.","q":"That is: ‘Take back whichever premiss you want’."},{"s":638,"ri":597,"ph":"I do not even know what I mean myself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":787,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus sees progress here: by admitting he does not know, Alcibiades has passed from double ignorance to simple ignorance.","q":"Notice how Alcibiades has clearly been benefitted: in fact, he has arrived from double ignorance to simple [ignorance], since he [now] agrees that he doesn’t know."},{"s":638,"ri":597,"ph":"I do not even know what I mean myself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades is more distressed here than at any earlier refutation, because he is shown ignorant of the goal of statesmanship, the subject that concerned him most.","q":"he is nowhere as distressed as he is here, because he has been proven ignorant about the very subject that concerned him most, that is, about the goal of the statesman’s knowledge:"},{"s":638,"ri":597,"ph":"in an utterly shameful condition","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":825,"ref":"Lecture 22","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades has been helped, since he now calls ignorance shameful, following the Sophist's distinction between the soul's defect, shamefulness, and the living creature's, disease.","q":"For there are two kinds of defectiveness ( kakia ), a point that has been demonstrated in the Sophist ; there is the defectiveness of the soul, and this is called ‘shamefulness’ ( aiskhos ) and ‘badness’ ( ponêria );"},{"s":639,"ri":598,"ph":"But you must take courage!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":789,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus likens Socrates to Hermes with his single rod: by dialectic he overthrows the proud and now raises up the fallen Alcibiades.","q":"so too Socrates uses one rod, namely dialectic, both to overthrow those who speak proudly, and to rouse again those who have fallen and call them back up."},{"s":639,"ri":598,"ph":"it would have been difficult for you to care for yourself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":789,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates says ‘difficult’, not ‘impossible’, for those of fifty, because he wants to help everyone, young and old.","q":"And the word ‘difficult’ was well chosen, but it’s not ‘impossible’: for Socrates wants to help every human being, both the young and the elderly."},{"s":639,"ri":600,"ph":"God wills it","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":794,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus takes ‘god’ here to mean Socrates' allotted daimon, whom Socrates had already called a god earlier in the dialogue.","q":"He says ‘god’ ( theos ) to refer to the allotted daimon"},{"s":639,"ri":600,"ph":"you trust in my prophecy","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":798,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates speaks of both kinds of prophecy: divine, implied by ‘if god wills’, and skilled, judging from Alcibiades' nature that he can learn.","q":"So Socrates is speaking now in terms of the double [nature] of prophecy;"},{"s":639,"ri":600,"ph":"you and I shall fare better","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":803,"ref":"Lecture 21","n":"Olympiodorus asks how Socrates too will be better off, and answers that he ascends together with his beloved.","q":"Clearly, it’s because he is ascending along with his beloved."},{"s":640,"ri":602,"ph":"let us ask what ‘care for yourself’ is","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":835,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus paraphrases: one must distinguish caring for oneself, for the self, and for one's belongings, lest in caring for externals we think we care for ourselves.","q":"He says, ‘We must understand what it is to care for or cultivate oneself ( to heautou epimeleisthai ), what it is to care for the self ( tou autou ), and third, what it is [to care] for one’s belongings, so that we don’t forget and imagine that, in caring for externals, we are caring for ourselves."},{"s":640,"ri":605,"ph":"I do not understand.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":835,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades' puzzlement: one who cares for his sandals seems to care for his feet as well.","q":"And [Alcibiades] replies that ‘I don’t understand ( ou manthanô )’, because the person who cares for his sandals seems to care for his feet as well."},{"s":640,"ri":606,"ph":"A ring, for instance","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":835,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates shifts to the ring as uncontroversial ground, since a ring plainly contributes nothing essential to the hand, so Alcibiades now answers readily.","q":"This is why Socrates transfers the argument to uncontroversial ground, by asking, ‘Does the person who cares for his ring also [thereby] care for his hand?’"},{"s":641,"ri":614,"ph":"whenever something is made better, do you call this ‘proper care’?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":835,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus says these words resolve the commentators' two puzzles: proper care is that which improves a thing's being, not merely its activity.","q":"Note how in these words he resolved the two puzzles, by saying that ‘I describe that person as “taking care” who makes the being ( ousia ) better, not its activity ( energeia )’."},{"s":642,"ri":624,"ph":"And is that not gymnastics?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":831,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus meets the objection that gymnastics and medicine, caring for the body, also aid the soul: they serve the soul's activity, not its being.","q":"[The solution is] that it is one thing to reflect on ( phrontisai ) oneself, and another to cultivate the soul with a view to its activity ( to energêsai )."},{"s":642,"ri":630,"ph":"to what belongs to the body by weaving and the other skills","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":832,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus answers the objection that weaving and sandal-making also serve the body: they serve its comfortable activity, whereas gymnastics and medicine attend to its being.","q":"[The solution is] again, that physical training and medicine, which reflect on ( phrontizousa ) the being ( ousia ) of the body, are one [kind of skill], while the skill which [reflects on] its activity (such as weaving and sandal-making) is another, which [operates] in order that [the body] may act comfortably ( eukolôs )."},{"s":644,"ri":642,"ph":"could we ever recognise what skill makes a shoe better, without knowing a shoe?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":833,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus calls this the second premiss: one must know a thing's being before caring for it, since different beings have different forms of care and fulfilment.","q":"[2] The second premiss is that someone who would care for anything must get to know its being well in advance: for in relation to different [kinds of] being, care and fulfilment ( teleiotês ) are different."},{"s":645,"ri":648,"ph":"this inscription in the temple at Delphi","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":20,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the inscription stood before the inner shrine so that those who came to consult the god about children, office or war would first think about and know themselves.","q":"it was therefore set in writing that they think about themselves and get to know themselves first, and the other things [about which they inquired] subsequently."},{"s":645,"ri":648,"ph":"this inscription in the temple at Delphi","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":834,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus says only ‘Know thyself’ was fittingly inscribed at Apollo's temple, since whoever knows himself knows all beings, and knowledge of all beings belongs to the prophet.","q":"And it was appropriate ( kalôs ) that before the temple of Apollo at Delphi all that was inscribed was ‘ Know Thyself ’, since the one who knows himself knows all beings, and the knowledge of all beings belongs to the prophet."},{"s":645,"ri":648,"ph":"was it some ordinary person who set up this inscription","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":851,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus takes ‘ordinary’ to mean lightweight, not bad in character, and names the author of the inscription as Chilon of Sparta, one of the seven sages.","q":"But it was Chilon of Lacedaemon."},{"s":645,"ri":648,"ph":"‘know thyself’","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":4,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, reads the Delphic inscription as showing the way back to the divine and the path of purification; whoever does not know himself is unfit to share Apollo's providence.","q":"so the inscription KNOW THYSELF, on the Delphic temple, manifests, as it appears to me, the mode of returning to a divine nature, and the most useful path to purification"},{"s":645,"ri":649,"ph":"I have often thought that it is for everybody, and often that it is extremely difficult","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":855,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus explains Alcibiades' wavering by a tragic verse: self-knowledge is a small thing in word but in deed known only to Zeus.","q":"To know yourself in words is nothing great; but in deed, only Zeus of all the gods knows [how to do it]."},{"s":645,"ri":650,"ph":"whether it is easy or difficult, here is where we stand anyway","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":858,"ref":"Lecture 23","n":"Olympiodorus paraphrases Socrates' reply: whether self-knowledge is hard or easy to attain, it remains absolutely necessary to know ourselves.","q":"For whether it is challenging or easy to know ourselves, it is absolutely necessary to know ourselves."},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"the self itself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":9,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' reading of the expression: 'self' means the tripartite soul, or simply the soul, while 'the self itself' means the rational soul.","q":"For Proclus says that 'self' is the tripartite soul ( hê trimereia tês psukhês ), or simply the soul, and that 'the self itself' is the rational soul"},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"the self itself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":9,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Damascius reads the expression differently: 'self' is the rational soul, and 'the self itself' the highest and most intellectual part of the soul.","q":"but Damascius approaches this differently and says that 'self' is the rational soul, while 'the self itself' the highest and most intellectual part of the soul."},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"by what means might we find the self itself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":872,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' reading: ‘self’ is the tripartite soul, ‘the self itself’ the rational soul, and ‘each self itself’ the individual.","q":"Now, the philosopher Proclus states that [the self] is three things: [he calls] the tripartition of the soul ‘self’ ( auto ); he calls the rational soul ‘the self itself’ ( auto to auto ); and [he calls] the individual ( atomon ) ‘each self itself’ ( auto to auto hekaston )."},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"by what means might we find the self itself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":874,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus reports Damascius' reading: ‘self’ is the civic soul using the body as instrument, ‘the self itself’ the purificatory and contemplative soul, which does not.","q":"But Damascius calls ‘self’ the civic soul, which uses the body as an instrument: and ‘the self itself’ [he calls] the purificatory and contemplative [soul], which certainly does not use the body as an instrument."},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"the self itself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":874,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus judges that Damascius is more scientific about the subject matter, while Proclus better fits the wording of the text.","q":"Thus [Damascius] attended to the [underlying] subject-matter ( pragmata) , whereas [Proclus] focused on the text ( lexis )."},{"s":646,"ri":652,"ph":"For in this way we may likely find what precisely we ourselves are","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":875,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus paraphrases: knowing what the human being in common is lets us know each individual, whereas without the common we cannot know the individual.","q":"‘If we could find out’, he says, ‘what the human being is, then in addition to this it’s also possible to know what each one of us is, for thus we might quickly learn about this [sc. each of us]; but ignorant of this, our common [humanity], it is impossible to get to know the individual’."},{"s":646,"ri":654,"ph":"Stop there, by Zeus!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":928,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus says the passage proves by induction that the human being uses the body as instrument: conversing uses speech, and using speech is using the body.","q":"[11] Hold on: The passage proves through induction that the human being uses the body as an instrument, if Socrates is conversing with Alcibiades, but a person who is having a conversation uses speech, and the person using speech as an instrument is using the body as an instrument;"},{"s":646,"ri":660,"ph":"And is Alcibiades the listener?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":876,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus treats Alcibiades' role as listener as pointing to a distinctive trait of the human being, the capacity to listen.","q":"and this is a distinctive trait ( idion ) of the human being, [the capacity] to listen’"},{"s":647,"ri":668,"ph":"a cobbler presumably cuts leather with a curved blade, a knife and other instruments","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":891,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes the two tools: the scraper has an even base, the knife a round one.","q":"( smilê ) has an even base, but the knife ( tomeus ) has a round base."},{"s":647,"ri":668,"ph":"a cobbler presumably cuts leather","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":887,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus says the cobbler example shows the whole body used as instrument, agreeing with the Peripatetic point that the hand does not weave, the human being weaves with his hand.","q":"weaves using his hand, since it is the whole [being] that acts through the part."},{"s":647,"ri":668,"ph":"a cobbler presumably cuts leather","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":921,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus notes that cobbling too is a work of the rational soul, since every skill is an echo of Intellect.","q":"[4] That cobbling is also the work of the rational [soul], since every skill ( tekhnê ) is an echo of Intellect ( nous )."},{"s":648,"ri":676,"ph":"Does he cut using the instruments alone or using his hands too?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":893,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus glosses ‘instruments’ as external tools and ‘hands’ as natural instruments, which the argument needs.","q":"[meaning] from outside, but ‘with hands’ meaning ‘with his natural instruments’,"},{"s":649,"ri":690,"ph":"So, is a person different from his own body?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":865,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus defends the premiss: a whole existing in its parts would not despise a part, yet a brave man gives up his body, so the human being is not the compound.","q":"it must be said that any whole, which possesses its real existence ( hupostasis ) in its parts, does not care nothing for ( periphronei ) the part, since [by doing so] it would aim at its own destruction:"},{"s":649,"ri":692,"ph":"Then what precisely is the person?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":925,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates makes the middle term of the first syllogism serve as the definition, the terms being convertible.","q":"Note how he wants the middle term of the first syllogism to serve as a definition, since he is also making the syllogism clear not only based on its own terms, but also based on what comes next, since it is built out of convertible terms."},{"s":649,"ri":693,"ph":"I cannot say.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":897,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades rightly hesitates over the definition, as he lacks the major premiss proving that the human being is the rational soul.","q":"missing the major premiss to prove that the human being is the rational soul:"},{"s":649,"ri":694,"ph":"he is the user of the body","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":12,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Damascius infers from this definition that the target is civic self-knowledge, since only the civic person uses the body as an instrument, needing spirit and appetite.","q":"And he establishes this from the definition of the human being in this dialogue as a rational soul ( psukhê logikê ) that uses the body as an instrument. Only the civic person uses the body as an instrument"},{"s":649,"ri":694,"ph":"he is the user of the body","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":864,"ref":"Lecture 24","n":"Olympiodorus tests the premiss and finds it seems to assume the conclusion, since using the body as an instrument is already distinctive of soul.","q":"That is, the object of inquiry is ‘Is the human being the soul’; and he has said that [the human being] uses the body as an instrument, which is distinctive ( idion ) of soul."},{"s":650,"ri":698,"ph":"In that case, does the soul rule?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":905,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus says the premiss that the human being rules the body is evident even from the incontinent, who could control themselves but do not, and so are blamed.","q":"and this [premiss] is evident not only from people who are in control ( tôn enkratôn ), but also from people who are out of control ( tôn akratôn ), since, though able to control [themselves], they do not control themselves:"},{"s":650,"ri":704,"ph":"Soul or body or both together, the whole.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":908,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus reports an objection that the same disjunction would prove honey-water to be water, and replies that the objector's premisses are false while Plato's are true.","q":"When the argument is advanced in this way, some criticise it by claiming that ‘on this argument, since honey-water is either water, or honey, or honey and water, and it is not honey, nor indeed honey and water, then what is left is that it is water’."},{"s":650,"ri":704,"ph":"Soul or body or both together, the whole.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":924,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus argues that for Plato the human being is not constituted out of soul and body, the living being arising rather from body and a non-rational image of soul.","q":"There is a great deal of outstanding evidence, therefore, that according to Plato the human being is not constituted out of the soul and body."},{"s":651,"ri":708,"ph":"Now, does the body itself rule itself?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":911,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus explains the denial: a body cannot rule a body, for it would then have to revert upon itself.","q":"[He asks] because it’s impossible for a body to rule a body, since [in that case] it would be [capable of] reverting upon itself."},{"s":651,"ri":714,"ph":"do both together rule the body","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":911,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus says the compound cannot rule as compound, for then the same absurdity would follow, that the body rules itself.","q":"[He asks] because the combination can’t rule insofar as it is a combination [of soul with body]. For the same paradoxical result ( atopon ) would recur, that the body would rule itself."},{"s":651,"ri":716,"ph":"It is the least likely of all!","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":927,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus takes this strong denial, with ‘it has been proven’, as evidence that Plato means his proofs that the human being is the soul in earnest.","q":"is proven by [his words] ‘unlikeliest of all’, which express strong disagreement ( diastatikos ), and [the phrase] ‘it’s been proven’."},{"s":652,"ri":718,"ph":"the person is nothing else but soul","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":910,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus says the resulting definition, rational soul using the body as instrument, must add ‘a body moving in a straight line’ to exclude the heavenly souls.","q":"And in order to prevent the definition also encompassing the heavenly souls, it is necessary to add ‘the body moving in a straight line ( to euthuphoroumenon sôma )’."},{"s":652,"ri":720,"ph":"the soul is the person","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":7,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' argument that Plato ends the discussion once this conclusion is reached, so that everything earlier serves to establish that the human being is the soul.","q":"And [he also argues] from the fact that [Plato] concludes the discussion once he has demonstrated, at the end of the dialogue, that the human being is the soul, on the ground that all of the prior assumptions are made for the purpose of establishing this [conclusion]."},{"s":652,"ri":720,"ph":"is it necessary to prove to you even more clearly that the soul is the person?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":911,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus reads the offer as one of more precise proofs concerning the purificatory, contemplative and inspired human being, which Alcibiades declines out of carelessness.","q":"And he replies, ‘It’s sufficient’, because he is careless ( rhâithumos )."},{"s":653,"ri":722,"ph":"not precisely but in good measure","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":911,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates himself admits these arguments are only rough, reaching the civic person by a stipulated definition rather than the inspired and higher persons.","q":"Note how he himself understands these arguments, which also emerge from a [stipulative] definition, and says that they are [only] loose or rough ( pakhumerês ), comparing the inspired person and the rest."},{"s":653,"ri":724,"ph":"we should first investigate the self itself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":911,"ref":"Lecture 25","n":"Olympiodorus says that here Damascius' reading prevails over Proclus': ‘self’ is the civic soul and ‘the self itself’ the purificatory and contemplative soul, still to be found.","q":"Previously the interpretation of the philosopher Proclus prevailed, that [Plato] calls the soul ‘self’ ( auto ), but the rational [soul] ‘self itself’; and now the [interpretation] of the philosopher Damascius [prevails], that ‘self’ is the civic soul, but ‘self itself’ is the purificatory and contemplative soul."},{"s":653,"ri":724,"ph":"instead of the self, we have considered what the person himself is","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":916,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates, seeking ‘self’, found only ‘each self’, the individual life, and therefore admits he has not found what he set out to find.","q":"And since, when his project was to discover ‘self’, he discovered ‘each self’, he indicates in what follows [130D] that he did not discover what he set out to [discover]."},{"s":653,"ri":724,"ph":"And perhaps this will suffice","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":922,"ref":"Lecture 26","n":"Olympiodorus finds ‘perhaps’ apt: the account of the civic person is enough for Alcibiades but not for Socrates.","q":"for the civic person suffices for Alcibiades, but not for Socrates."},{"s":653,"ri":726,"ph":"using words by means of soul, directed to soul","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":933,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus says ‘conversation’ is now reinterpreted: since Socrates and Alcibiades are souls, it is souls conversing with each other, a corollary of the argument.","q":"But now he offers the following sort of interpretation ( dianoia ) of the same [word] ‘conversation’: ‘Since Socrates is conversing with Alcibiades, and they have been proven to be souls, the souls are conversing with one another with the aim of mutual intercourse’ [139D]:"},{"s":654,"ri":730,"ph":"whoever is commanding us to 'know thyself' is calling upon us to know the soul","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":21,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus argues that the injunction itself reveals that the human being is the rational soul, since it is addressed to what can know, not to the body, the compound or the vegetative soul.","q":"And through the injunction, [the Delphic inscription] revealed what was enjoined: that the human being is the rational soul. For [the inscription] says 'Know Thyself', and this was addressed to the soul, which has a capacity for knowledge ( gnôstikos )."},{"s":654,"ri":730,"ph":"whoever is commanding us to 'know thyself' is calling upon us to know the soul","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":22,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus weighs each word: 'thyself' excludes the non-rational soul, which cannot revert upon itself, and 'know' excludes the heavenly beings, whose knowledge is never a passage from ignorance.","q":"for in saying 'Know', [the inscription] speaks of being transformed from ignorance to knowledge, namely a [process of] fulfilment ( teleiotês ) for the soul, whereas those [heavenly beings] always know [and so their knowledge is already complete]."},{"s":654,"ri":730,"ph":"is calling upon us to know the soul","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":6,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, makes this knowledge of our own essence the most perfect and leading design of the whole dialogue, so that the care and knowledge of ourselves is its end.","q":"But the most perfect and leading design of the whole conversation is the speculation of our own essence. So that he will not err who establishes the care and knowledge of ourselves, as the end of the dialogue."},{"s":656,"ri":736,"ph":"So farmers and other craftsmen fall far short of knowing themselves.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":934,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus calls this the second corollary: no expert in a particular craft, as such, has self-knowledge, since crafted things have no rational principles in the soul.","q":"[2] The second corollary [is] that no expert in the particular skilled crafts [insofar as he is such an expert] has self-knowledge."},{"s":656,"ri":738,"ph":"if sound-mindedness is knowing thyself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":935,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus asks why self-knowledge belongs to self-control rather than practical wisdom, and answers that reversion belongs to self-control.","q":"Well, it is because reversion belongs to self-control: for when [the soul] is engaged in the worst sort [of behaviour] and cannot descend further, it ascends."},{"s":656,"ri":738,"ph":"none of them are sound-minded on account of their skill","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":936,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus distinguishes three kinds of self-control and says craftsmen, like statesmen, can have only the civic kind, the reversion of the non-rational to reason.","q":"And the reversion of the non-rational to reason is civic self-control ( politikê sophrosunê ), and this is the only self-control that craftsmen skilled in particulars and statesmen ( politikoi ) alike are capable of having;"},{"s":657,"ri":748,"ph":"he has not actually loved Alcibiades but something belonging to Alcibiades","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":937,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus calls this the fourth corollary: the other lovers loved Alcibiades' belongings, and Socrates alone, loving his soul, is properly his lover.","q":"[4] The fourth corollary: [that] none of Alcibiades’ other lovers loves him , but [rather loves] one of Alcibiades’ belongings; but Socrates alone loves Alcibiades and is properly speaking his ‘lover’:"},{"s":658,"ri":757,"ph":"May you never leave me.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus sees in Alcibiades' plea that Socrates should never leave him the clear display of his returning love.","q":"Notice how he entreats [Socrates] to remain: his reciprocation of love ( anterôs ) is clearly displayed by this."},{"s":658,"ri":758,"ph":"Then you should yearn to be as beautiful as possible.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus explains that love, ‘formidable’ and ‘heroic’ as its name suggests, cannot bear a worthless object and wants the beloved to be lofty.","q":"For love ( erôs ), since it is ‘formidable’ ( errhômenos ) (which is also why it has obtained this name), and since it is ‘heroic’ ( hêrôikos ), cannot bear an object without value ( eutelês pragma ), but wants the beloved to be lofty."},{"s":659,"ri":760,"ph":"Phaenarete","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":41,"ref":"Lecture 2","n":"Olympiodorus links Socrates' mother Phaenarete, a midwife, with his midwifery of souls: he brings ideas to birth rather than putting theories into the young.","q":"which is also why they say that he is the son of Phainarete, who was a midwife ( maia ) – like Hermes [the son of Maia]."},{"s":659,"ri":760,"ph":"save one only, a person worthy of love, Socrates","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus removes the apparent redundancy of a sole lover being ‘cherished’ by supplying the omitted article: the one lover is the cherished Socrates.","q":"Well, it is necessary to understand the phrase as follows: ‘You have come to have one lover, and that is the cherished’ (for he omits the article) ‘Socrates, the son of the midwife Phaenarete and the stonemason Sophroniscus’:"},{"s":660,"ri":764,"ph":"I was the only lover of you, the others loved what belongs to you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":5,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports Proclus' argument that, since the others loved a possession of Alcibiades and Socrates alone loved Alcibiades himself, the dialogue is about the self.","q":"And [he argues] from the statement that all who were in love with the body of Alcibiades did not love Alcibiades [himself], but rather one of his possessions, and it was Socrates alone who loved Alcibiades himself."},{"s":660,"ri":764,"ph":"unless you are corrupted by the Athenian populace","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":938,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus explains that the people are a terrible teacher, since by praising orators they lead them to make speeches that please the crowd.","q":"[He says this] because the people are a terrifying ( deinos ) teacher, since, by praising their orators, they encourage them to construct speeches that are pleasing to the people."},{"s":660,"ri":764,"ph":"many good Athenians have suffered this fate before you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus asks how good men could be corrupted by the people, and answers that they were good only in disposition, not in practice.","q":"If they [were] good, how did they become lovers of the people, and suffer ruin? Well, they were only good in their disposition ( hexis ) [and not in practice]."},{"s":660,"ri":764,"ph":"you need to see it naked","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":939,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus calls this stripping Socrates' moly: he strips away the multitude, like tunics, so that Alcibiades sees the people as the cobblers and fullers they are.","q":"And [Socrates] calls the medicine not ‘moly’, but ‘stripping off’: for he strips the multiplicity ( plêthos ) from [Alcibiades], like multiple tunics."},{"s":661,"ri":766,"ph":"Thus you may proceed in possession of an antidote","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":938,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus compares Socrates to Hermes giving Odysseus moly against Circe, here the people, so that Alcibiades is not turned into a beast.","q":"And what Hermes is for Odysseus, Socrates becomes for Alcibiades: when [Alcibiades] is about to approach the people (corresponding to Circe), he gives him the antidote ( pharmakon alexêtêrion ) called ‘moly’;"},{"s":661,"ri":766,"ph":"having first exercised and learned what needs to be learned","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":939,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus calls this a second antidote, the care of excellence, assigning training to the non-rational part and learning to reason.","q":"But [Socrates] also provides [Alcibiades] with another antidote: for he advises him to care for excellence by training and learning; and training befits the non-rational [part of the soul], but learning [befits] reason."},{"s":661,"ri":767,"ph":"try to explain in what way we may care for ourselves","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus says the doubled expression ‘our own selves’ points to ‘the self itself’, the purificatory and contemplative life, the discussion having so far concerned the civic life.","q":"And the phrase ‘our own selves’ ( hêmôn autôn ) points by duplication [of the words] to ‘the self itself’, the purificatory life and the contemplative [life]: for up until this point, the civic life [has been under discussion]."},{"s":662,"ri":774,"ph":"So in what way may we know it with the utmost clarity?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus finds ‘clearest’ fitting for the command ‘Know thyself’, which he links with the Sun, the overseer of clarity.","q":"The [word] ‘clearest’ ( enargestata ) is appropriate to the [command to] ‘Know Thyself’, which is the Sun, who is the overseer ( ephoros ) of clarity ( enargeia )."},{"s":663,"ri":776,"ph":"only the example based upon sight","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus explains that sight is the most fitting example because it is sun-like and so suits the god who gives the command.","q":"That is, ‘There isn’t any more appropriate example than sight, because it is sun-like in form ( hêlioeides ), and appropriate to the one who makes the command [to know oneself]’."},{"s":663,"ri":778,"ph":"it said ‘see thyself’","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":940,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus applies the image: as the eye cannot revert on itself and must look into another eye, so Alcibiades must look into Socrates' soul and know his own through it.","q":"and likewise you, since you have utterly blinded what is self-moving in you and acted as something externally moved and lacking the capacity to revert upon yourself, now look into my soul, and know your own [soul] through it."},{"s":664,"ri":781,"ph":"when we look at mirrors and the like","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus takes ‘mirrors’ as artificial mirrors and ‘things of that sort’ as natural ones such as crystal and horn.","q":"Through the [word] ‘mirrors’ he refers to artificial mirrors, and through the [phrase] ‘things of that sort’, to natural [mirrors], such as crystal and horn."},{"s":665,"ri":784,"ph":"we actually call the pupil ‘a doll’","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":943,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus explains the name korê, maiden, for the pupil: it is pure like crystal and an unmarried girl, or it is wrapped in many protective layers like a maiden.","q":"And we should recognise that [the word] ‘maiden’ [‘pupil’] ( korê ) is used either because of its proximity to the form of crystal, which is pure, and so is the maiden, since she is unmarried; or it’s because it has been wrapped around with many protective layers, just like the maiden."},{"s":665,"ri":784,"ph":"the features of the person looking into the eye appear in the pupil","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":941,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Against Proclus, Olympiodorus follows Damascius: images in mirrors are not reflections but real copies formed by outflows, as one sees oneself in a picture without reflection.","q":"For, as the philosopher Damascius says, just as the person who sees himself in a picture ( eikôn ) does not see himself by reflection ( anaklasis ), so too he does not see himself by reflection in the mirror; but [he sees himself there] because there is a copy ( apeikonisma ) of the eye there."},{"s":665,"ri":786,"ph":"looking into the best part of it, the part with which it too is seeing","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":19,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reads the eye analogy as embracing all grades of self-knowledge: looking to Socrates is civic, and looking not to any random part but the highest is purificatory.","q":"Now by saying 'look away to me', he has indicated that the target of the dialogue is self-knowledge in a civic sense; and by saying 'not to any random part,' that it is also [self-knowledge] in a purificatory sense, for self-purification is the province of the highest part of the soul"},{"s":667,"ri":796,"ph":"having known all that is divine, both god and wisdom","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":19,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus takes seeing intellect in the soul as contemplative self-knowledge and seeing god as inspired self-knowledge, since we are inspired according to the simple divine element in us.","q":"and through saying 'and god', [he has indicated] that it is also [self-knowledge] in an inspired sense, for we are inspired according to the divine in us, which is simple, just as is the divine itself."},{"s":667,"ri":796,"ph":"having known all that is divine, both god and wisdom","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":940,"ref":"Lecture 27","n":"Olympiodorus says that in Socrates' soul Alcibiades will find divine images, Intellect and God, acting purificatorily by Intellect and contemplatively by God.","q":"And when you look away to my [soul], you will find therein ‘divine images ( theia agalmata )’, Intellect ( nous ) and God ( theos ); and in virtue of Intellect you will act in a purificatory manner, whereas in virtue of God you will act in a contemplative manner."},{"s":667,"ri":802,"ph":"Did we agree that to ‘know thyself’ is sound-mindedness?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus explains that self-control suits reversion because it governs appetite, the part furthest from reason, from which the soul can only turn back to its betters.","q":"For it was also pointed out earlier that self-control is appropriate to reversion, because it rides upon appetite ( epithumia ), around the part [of the soul] most remote [from reason]."},{"s":668,"ri":806,"ph":"to you it probably seems impossible","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus finds the solution to the earlier puzzle in ‘perhaps’: it is only perhaps impossible for a doctor to know his belongings without knowing himself.","q":"Note the solution to the puzzle in the word ‘perhaps’, since it is ‘perhaps’ impossible that the doctor know his belongings, yet not himself."},{"s":668,"ri":810,"ph":"we do not know what belongs to our belongings either","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus calls this step superfluous, the first claim sufficing, but says Socrates adds it to hint again at the solution, ignorance of belongings in their relation to us.","q":"This is an unnecessary addition ( ek perittou ), that the person who is ignorant of his own belongings is also ignorant of what belongs to those belongings: for the first [assertion] sufficed;"},{"s":669,"ri":812,"ph":"what belongs to himself, and what belongs to the belongings of himself","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":5,"ref":"Lecture 1","n":"Olympiodorus reports that Proclus takes this threefold distinction of self, what belongs to self, and what belongs to one's belongings as evidence that the dialogue's target is self-knowledge.","q":"And in addition in the dialogue before us the following three designations are distinguished: 'me', 'what belongs to me', and 'what belongs to my belongings'."},{"s":669,"ri":812,"ph":"So we were not entirely correct when we agreed earlier","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates here corrects the earlier claim that the doctor and money-maker know their belongings without themselves, since one person knows self, belongings and their belongings.","q":"For he says that ‘We weren’t right to say that the money-maker and the doctor know their belongings and their belongings’ belongings without knowing themselves: for one and the same person knows me, what’s mine, and what belongs to what’s mine’."},{"s":669,"ri":814,"ph":"be ignorant of what belongs to others","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus grounds the step in the principle that knowledge of opposites is one and the same.","q":"The second premiss: [it follows] because knowledge of opposites is one and the same."},{"s":669,"ri":818,"ph":"Then a man like this could never become a statesman.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus notes that Socrates raises the tone in the conclusion, as orators do at their climaxes.","q":"In the conclusion, he raises the tone of the argument by saying that such a man is unable to be a statesman, that is, ‘he is not able to benefit from the good that you have’."},{"s":671,"ri":838,"ph":"the cities need neither walls nor triremes nor dockyards","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":947,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus takes the mention of walls, triremes and dockyards as a veiled allusion to Pericles, who built the Piraeus wall, and Themistocles, who prepared the triremes.","q":"And he says this to point riddlingly to Pericles, who built the wall of the Piraeus, and Themistocles, who prepared the triremes, by the oracle:"},{"s":672,"ri":846,"ph":"Then you should make justice and sound-mindedness available","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates names only justice and self-control because Alcibiades already had practical wisdom as a statesman and courage as a general.","q":"And he mentioned these excellences because [Alcibiades] possessed the others (since he had practical wisdom, as an organiser of the city’s affairs, and courage, as a general)."},{"s":673,"ri":850,"ph":"looking to what is divine and bright","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus takes ‘bright’ to signify the intellectual element in us, set against the ‘godless and dark’ that follows.","q":"And through the word ‘bright’, he pointed to the intelligence [in us] ( to noeron ); and through what follows, he ranged against them the ‘godless and dark’."},{"s":674,"ri":857,"ph":"You are indeed a secure guarantor.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":955,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus says Socrates is a safe guarantor because whoever has knowledge in reasoned arguments is one; he guarantees that the good man fares well and the bad man badly.","q":"And Socrates is a ‘safe guarantor’ because the one who has knowledge in reasoned arguments ( logoi ) is a safe guarantor."},{"s":675,"ri":860,"ph":"Is it not likely that his body will be ruined?","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus draws the third argument: such a man has not power but powerlessness, since no genuine power destroys its possessor.","q":"[Here is] the third [argument], that [in such a case] there is not even power, but powerlessness, because no [genuine] power is destructive to its possessor."},{"s":676,"ri":868,"ph":"to be ruled by someone better rather than exercising rule","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus says the good man's slavery to god is superior to any rule, since being moved by god in this way is better than self-movement.","q":"Well, this slavery is superior to every [kind of] rule, and [this person], by his slavery, rules over all others: for the condition of being moved by another ( heterokinêtos ) in this way is better than self-movement ( autokinêtos )."},{"s":677,"ri":884,"ph":"Let us not name it in the case of a noble man.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus supplies the unspoken word, ‘slavishness’, and says namelessness suits matter, which is ugly and formless.","q":"The matter is full of what isn’t said, namely, ‘slavishness’: for namelessness is suitable to matter, which is ugly and without form."},{"s":678,"ri":890,"ph":"If God wills it.","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus reconciles both answers: excellences are human as excellences, but as achieving well-being they come from god.","q":"For the excellences are human insofar as they are excellences, but as concerned with achieving well-being, they derive from god: thus both [answers] are true, Alcibiades’ and Socrates’."},{"s":678,"ri":891,"ph":"we are likely to exchange roles","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus sees reciprocal love appearing here: Alcibiades foresees that their roles will be exchanged, he becoming the lover and Socrates the beloved.","q":"for he says that ‘it’s likely that we’ll exchange the arrangement [of our relationship], as I become the lover, and you the beloved’."},{"s":679,"ri":892,"ph":"my love will turn out to be just like a stork","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":42,"ref":"Lecture 2","n":"Olympiodorus explains the image: storks are fed by their parents and later feed them in old age, just as lover and beloved exchange roles at the dialogue's end.","q":"At the end, too, [Socrates] concludes the dialogue by comparing his love to the storks: since the storks too, having first been nourished by their parents, finally nourish them in their old age, and in this way they change [their respective positions]."},{"s":679,"ri":892,"ph":"my love will turn out to be just like a stork","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":954,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus explains the stork image: storks care for their aged parents, bathing and carrying them, and so Alcibiades returns the care he received.","q":"for just as storks ( pelargoi ) take care of their parents when they have grown old, bathe them and carry them, and simply accomplish everything that their parents did for themselves, so too Alcibiades here imitates the stork."},{"s":679,"ri":892,"ph":"the winged love it hatched within you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":954,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus links the winged love to the Phaedrus, where love has wings, and derives the name ‘Winged’ (pterôs) from pteron, wing.","q":"but also because love ‘has wings’, as [Plato] says in the Phaedrus ; that is also why he is called ‘Winged’ ( pterôs ), deriving from ‘wing’ ( pteron )."},{"s":679,"ri":892,"ph":"my love will turn out to be just like a stork","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":7,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, as Taylor reports, sees the amatory aim fulfilled as Socrates perfects Alcibiades until the young man becomes a lover of his providential care, the chief good of the amatory art.","q":"he proceeds perfecting the young man till he renders him a lover of his providential care, which is the leading good of the amatory art"},{"s":679,"ri":893,"ph":"I shall begin henceforth to care for justice","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus says Alcibiades resolves to be just after hearing of the stork, because the stork's life is just.","q":"After he has heard about the stork, he says, ‘I will begin to be just’, because [their] life is just."},{"s":679,"ri":894,"ph":"I fear that it may overpower both me and you","c":"Olympiodorus","e":"late-antiquity","y":560,"w":"Commentary on the First Alcibiades","k":"olymp-alc1","p":957,"ref":"Lecture 28","n":"Olympiodorus asks how the people could overpower Socrates too, and answers that it will overcome him as a lover, together with his beloved.","q":"How does the people overcome Socrates as well? The answer is that it will overcome him as a lover, and with his beloved."}]});
